/*
 * AG LAB · long report copy, part 1: type portraits, preferences, functions, paths in depth.
 * Every string is { zh, en }. For self-reflection; nothing here predicts or promises anything.
 */
(function (root) {
  "use strict";
  var L = root.LAB || (root.LAB = {});

  /* ---------------- type portraits: portrait, thinking, others, pressure, growth ---------------- */
  L.typeDeep = {
    INTJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你往往先看见整体：整场展览、整套系列，或者作品最终要提出的论点。材料和技法对你来说，主要是抵达这个构想的路径。你可能会花上几周阅读、画关系图，墙上却还空着；而一旦作品出现，通常已经带着清晰的结构。你的作品经得起细看，因为几乎每个决定背后都有理由。", en: "As a maker, you tend to start from a picture of the whole: the finished show, the full series, the argument the work will make. Materials and techniques matter to you mainly as ways of reaching that vision. You may spend weeks reading and mapping before anything appears on the wall, and when it does, it often arrives with a clear structure. Your work rewards close looking, because almost every decision has a reason behind it." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常是从终点倒推：先想清楚作品要落在哪里，再拆出每一步，删掉不服务于核心概念的东西。你很容易在不相干的资料之间看出规律，一本科普书里的图表，可能就解开了构图上的难题。风险在于计划定得太早、太硬。不妨在速写本里专门留一页“走错的路”，让有用的意外继续留在视野里。", en: "On a project, your mind often works backwards from an end point. You picture where the piece needs to land, then reverse-engineer the steps, cutting anything that does not serve the core idea. Patterns jump out at you across unrelated sources: a diagram in a popular science book may solve a composition problem. The risk is that the plan hardens too early, so a sketchbook page labelled “wrong turns” can keep useful accidents in play." },
      others: { zh: "和同学在一起时，你常常是那个追问“这个项目到底为了什么”的人。导师可能欣赏你的清晰，但在评图时，你也许显得比自己实际更笃定。小组合作里，你倾向于负责搭框架，把人际协调留给别人，事先说明分工效果最好。观众通常会被作品的完整性打动，一段简短、平实的展签能帮他们走进去。", en: "With peers, you are often the one who asks what the project is actually for. Tutors may value your clarity, though in a crit you might come across as more certain than you feel. In group work, you tend to take on the structure and leave the social glue to others, which works best when you agree the split openly. Audiences usually respond to the coherence of your work; a short, plain-language wall text helps them in." },
      pressure: { zh: "临近截止时，你可能会退回自己的世界，想一个人把所有问题都解决，于是不断修改概念，而不是把作品做完。尖锐的评图意见，也可能让你觉得整个体系都出了错，冲动之下想推倒重来。这时更有效的做法是把问题缩小：列出仍然关键的三个决定，做完它们，其余的交稿之后再打磨。离开屏幕走一走，往往能让注意力重新聚焦。", en: "Under deadline pressure, you may retreat inward and try to fix everything alone, reworking the concept rather than finishing the piece. Harsh critique can feel like proof that the whole system is flawed, and you might be tempted to start again from scratch. What usually helps is shrinking the problem: list the three decisions that still matter, make them, and accept that the rest can be refined after the hand-in. A short walk away from screens often resets your focus." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是让作品在“还没准备好”时就被看见。长时间独自打磨，会让每次展示都压力很大，反馈也来得太晚，改不动了。可以试一个两周一次的小仪式：钉出一张粗糙的测试稿，只问同学一个具体问题，把回答原样记进速写本，先不辩解。一个学期下来，你可能会发现，早一点的不完美曝光，反而让你的构想更锋利。", en: "Your growth edge is letting the work be seen before it feels ready. Long private development can make each showing feel high-stakes, so feedback arrives too late to change anything. Try a fortnightly ritual: pin up one rough test, ask a peer one specific question about it, and write their answer in your sketchbook without replying. Over a term, you may notice that early, imperfect exposure sharpens your vision rather than diluting it." }
    },
    INTP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你总被题目底下那个问题吸引。你喜欢把一个想法拆开，看清它怎么运作，再用更奇特、也更精确的方式重组。你的速写本可能更像实验笔记，满是箭头、定义和做了一半的模型。最后的作品往往以一种安静的方式显得原创：它不喧哗，却会让人重新思考某件原本习以为常的事。", en: "As a maker, you are drawn to the question underneath the brief. You like to take an idea apart to see how it works, then rebuild it in a stranger, more precise form. Your sketchbooks may look more like lab notes than drawings, full of arrows, definitions and half-finished models. The work that results is often original in a quiet way: it does not shout, but it makes people rethink something they took for granted." },
      thinking: { zh: "你的思路常常从检验定义开始。动手之前，你可能会追问“肖像”或“家”到底指什么，题目里有没有藏着某个默认的前提。你经常在脑中同时摆着好几种模型，并享受找出每一个漏洞的过程。这让你的思考很严密，但也容易停在原地，因为下一个有趣的问题永远在等着。在每页速写本顶端写一句假设，能让探索保持方向。", en: "Your mind tends to work by testing definitions. Before committing, you may ask what “portrait” or “home” really means, and whether the brief hides an assumption. You often hold several models in your head at once and enjoy finding the flaw in each. This makes your thinking rigorous, but it can also stall, because the next interesting question is always waiting. Writing a one-line hypothesis at the top of each page keeps the enquiry pointed." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是一个大方的讨论对手，愿意为了想法本身辩上很久。导师可能喜欢你提的问题，但也希望看到更多与之相配的实际制作。小组项目里，你也许会自然地接下别人不想碰的技术或理论难题，只要清楚地向大家汇报，这就很有价值。观众有时需要一个入口，一个标题或一句背景说明，就能把谜题变成邀请。", en: "With peers, you are often a generous sparring partner, happy to debate an idea for its own sake. Tutors may enjoy your questions but want to see more making alongside them. In groups, you might drift towards the technical or theoretical problem nobody else wants, which is valuable if you report back clearly. Audiences sometimes need a way in; a title or a single sentence of context can turn a puzzle into an invitation." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会继续查资料而迟迟不做决定，期待再多一份文献就能让答案自己浮现。听到不合逻辑的评图意见时，你也许会烦躁，忍不住当场争辩，而不是先消化。比较有用的是把两件事分开：给思考固定的一小时，再给制作一段不再接收新信息的时间。评图之后，先把每条意见原样记下来，第二天冷静时再判断它们是否成立。", en: "Under pressure, you may keep researching instead of deciding, hoping one more source will make the answer obvious. Critique that feels illogical can irritate you, and you might argue the point rather than absorb it. What helps is separating the two tasks: give yourself a fixed hour to think, then a fixed block to make with no new inputs. After a crit, write down every comment first and judge its logic the next day, when you are calmer." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方是“完成”。一个想法一旦被你想通，就仿佛已经结束了，真正可见的成品反而成了次要的事。可以每个月给自己定一个小小的公开节点：一本小志、一张版画，或者给朋友看的两分钟演示。先定日期，再定内容。慢慢地，你可能会发现，一件做完的作品能教给你的东西，是再多思考也换不来的。", en: "Your growth edge is finishing. Ideas can feel complete to you once you understand them, so the final, visible version may seem like an afterthought. Try setting yourself a small, public endpoint each month: a zine, a single print, a two-minute demo shown to friends. Decide the date first and the content second. Over time, you may find that a finished piece teaches you things that no amount of thinking could reveal." }
    },
    ENTJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你习惯从结果出发：作品要达成什么、给谁看、什么时候完成。在环节众多、野心很大的项目里，你往往状态最好，因为定方向的能力本身就成了作品的一部分。你的作品集可能呈现出规模感和自信，在形式与呈现上都有清楚的取舍。你的创作目标明确，身边的人通常也很清楚你想抵达哪里。", en: "As a maker, you tend to think in terms of outcomes: what the work must achieve, for whom, and by when. You are often at your best on ambitious projects with many moving parts, where your ability to set direction becomes part of the work itself. Your portfolio may show scale and confidence, with clear decisions about format and presentation. The making is purposeful, and people around you usually know exactly what you are aiming for." },
      thinking: { zh: "你的思路常常像一张制作排期表。拿到题目，你可能会先切成几个阶段，定下大致的节点，然后寻找通往有说服力结果的最快路线。你擅长发现哪里低效、哪里不清楚，也倾向于尽早拍板，让项目往前走。风险在于想法还没长成，选项就已经被关掉了。不妨在每份计划里，刻意留出一周完全开放的时间。", en: "Your mind often works like a production schedule. You may break a brief into phases, assign rough deadlines and look for the quickest route to a convincing result. You are good at spotting what is inefficient or unclear, and you tend to make decisions early so the project can move. The risk is closing down options before the idea has fully formed, so build one deliberately open week into every plan." },
      others: { zh: "和同学在一起时，就算没人开口，你也常常自然成了组织者。很多人欣赏你带来的推进力，也有人可能觉得被催促、没被听见。导师通常尊重你的干劲，同时会在深度上追问你。合作时，在拍板之前主动问问比较安静的成员怎么想，结果可能大不一样。观众看到的往往是你最完整的作品，适当露出一点探索的痕迹，会更动人。", en: "With peers, you often become the organiser, even when nobody asked. Many people appreciate the momentum; some may feel rushed or unheard. Tutors usually respect your drive and will push you on depth. In collaborations, explicitly asking quieter members for their view before you decide can transform the result. Audiences tend to meet your work at its most finished and polished, a strength worth pairing with a few visible traces of exploration." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会把一切都揽到自己手里，把计划收得更紧，并期待别人跟上你的节奏。如果评图意见质疑你的方向，你也许会把它当成需要清除的障碍，而不是有用的信息。这时可以先停一下再回应，追问一句，把对方的意思弄清楚。真正交出一项重要任务，也能减轻负担。睡眠和吃饭往往最先被你牺牲，也最值得守住。", en: "Under pressure, you may take control of everything, tightening the plan and expecting others to keep pace. Critique that questions your direction can feel like an obstacle to remove rather than information. What usually helps is pausing before you respond and asking one follow-up question to understand the comment fully. Delegating a real task, not just a small one, also eases the load. Sleep and meals tend to be the first things you cut, and the first worth protecting." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是在不确定里多停留一会儿，让意料之外的东西长出来。并非创作的每个阶段都需要计划，一些最好的作品恰恰来自缓慢、漫无目的的玩耍。可以每周安排九十分钟无目标的时间：只用一种材料，不拍照，也不要求成果，做出来的东西放进盒子而不是作品集。久而久之，这些时间可能会带来单靠策略想不到的点子。", en: "Your growth edge is staying with uncertainty long enough for something unexpected to emerge. Not every stage of making benefits from a plan, and some of the strongest work arrives through slow, aimless play. Try a weekly ninety-minute session with no goal: one material, no photographs, no outcome to show. Keep the results in a box rather than your portfolio. Over time, those sessions may feed your larger projects with ideas that strategy alone would never produce." }
    },
    ENTP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，最能点燃你的是一件作品可能引发的争论。你喜欢把题目反过来想，从意想不到的领域借方法，看两个想法撞在一起会发生什么。你的速写本里可能挤满了挑衅、玩笑和停不下来的图解。状态好的时候，你的作品生动、出人意料，还带点调皮，邀请观众去质疑，而不只是欣赏。", en: "As a maker, you are energised by the argument a piece can start. You enjoy flipping a brief on its head, borrowing methods from unlikely fields and seeing what happens when two ideas collide. Your sketchbook may be crowded with provocations, jokes and diagrams that rarely sit still. At its best, your work is lively, surprising and a little mischievous, inviting viewers to question something rather than simply admire it." },
      thinking: { zh: "你的思路往往会不断分叉。一个问题引出三个新问题，第一个方向还没验证，你可能已经在探索下一个了。这让你前期的研究丰富又出人意料。难点在于取舍，因为你常常看到每个选项的优点。一个有用的方法是：给每个想法做一次快速、粗糙的测试，再把它们并排钉在同一面墙上，选那个依然让你惊讶的。", en: "Your mind tends to branch. One question opens three more, and you may find yourself exploring a new direction before the first has been tested. This makes your early research rich and unexpected. The challenge is choosing, because you often see the merit in every option. A useful method is to give each idea a quick, rough test, then pin the results side by side on one wall and commit to the one that still surprises you." },
      others: { zh: "和同学讨论时，你常常是点火的人，用反驳来检验一个想法。有些同学觉得很过瘾，也有人可能听成了否定，所以不妨直接说明“我先唱个反调”。导师通常喜欢你的活力，也会追问你的落实在哪里。小组项目里，你擅长提案和临场转向。当你的挑衅落在一件认真做好的实物上时，观众往往最容易被打动。", en: "With peers, you are often the spark in a discussion, testing ideas by arguing against them. Some classmates find this thrilling; others may hear it as dismissal, so saying “let me play devil’s advocate” out loud helps. Tutors usually enjoy your energy and will ask where the follow-through is. In group projects, you are strong at the pitch and the pivot. Audiences tend to respond when your provocation is grounded in one carefully made object." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会开一个新坑，而不是做完手上的东西，因为新鲜感让人觉得有进展。听到批评时，你也许会想辩论，而那时倾听可能更有用。比较有效的是一个看得见的公开承诺：明确告诉一位同学你会交什么、什么时候交。把剩下的工作拆成短冲刺，冒出来的新点子先记在单独的“以后再说”清单里，不急着去做。", en: "Under pressure, you may start something new instead of finishing what is in front of you, because novelty feels like progress. Critique can trigger a debate when listening might serve you better. What often helps is a visible, public commitment: tell a peer exactly what you will hand in and when. Break the remaining work into short sprints, and note new ideas on a separate “later” list rather than acting on them straight away." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方是“深度”。很多方向都能让你兴奋，但当你把其中一个坚持得比自己习惯的更久时，作品才会真正丰厚起来。可以试着整个学期只追一个问题，围绕它做十个变体，每一版都往前推一点，并记录每一版之间改变了什么。到学期末，你可能会发现，持续的专注能让你的挑衅拥有快点子很少具备的分量。", en: "Your growth edge is depth. Many directions excite you, but the work becomes richer when one is pursued for longer than feels natural. Try choosing a single question for a whole term and producing ten variations on it, each pushing a little further. Keep a log of what changed between versions. By the end, you may find that sustained attention gives your provocations a weight and authority that quick ideas rarely achieve." }
    },
    INFJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你会被表面之下的东西吸引：一件物品的意义，一个地方藏着的故事，人们难以说出口的感受。你常常把个人经验连接到关于记忆、归属或关怀的更大问题上。你的作品可能层次丰富、安静而有力量，第二次看时会发现更多。人们往往能感觉到，它是带着耐心和强烈的内在使命感做出来的。", en: "As a maker, you are drawn to what lies beneath the surface: the meaning of an object, the story a place holds, the feeling people find hard to say. You often connect personal experience to larger questions about memory, belonging or care. Your work may be layered and quietly intense, revealing more on a second visit. People often sense that it was made with patience and a strong inner sense of purpose." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常需要“孵化”。你可能会很长时间地收集图像、笔记和对话，然后某个清晰的想法才突然成形，有时就在一瞬间。它一旦出现，你通常会非常信任它。风险在于为了等这一刻而等得太久。在孵化阶段做一些小而低风险的习作，能让你的直觉有具体的东西可以回应，也可能让洞见来得更快。", en: "On a project, your mind often works by incubation. You may gather images, notes and conversations for a long time before a single, clear idea crystallises, sometimes all at once. Once it arrives, you tend to trust it deeply. The risk is waiting too long for that moment. Making small, low-stakes studies during the incubation phase gives your intuition something concrete to react to and can bring the insight sooner." },
      others: { zh: "和同学在一起时，你常是一位敏锐的倾听者，能察觉别人在作品里真正想表达的东西。导师可能觉得你深思熟虑、乐于接受意见，但你也许为了让气氛平和，把最强烈的观点藏了起来。合作时，你往往最早感觉到团队里的紧张。观众常常觉得你的作品在对自己说话，为他们留一处安静的空间或阅读角，能让这种连接更深。", en: "With peers, you are often a perceptive listener who notices what someone is really trying to say in their work. Tutors may find you thoughtful and receptive, though you might hold back your strongest opinions to keep the room calm. In collaborations, you tend to sense tensions early. Audiences often feel personally addressed by your work; giving them a quiet space or a reading corner can deepen that connection." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会在“意义”上变得完美主义，觉得作品必须一次把所有东西都说出来。评图意见哪怕很温和，也可能在你心里停留好几天，你也许会躲起来独自消化。这时比较有用的是只说清这件作品关于的一件事，其余的留给以后的作品。和一位信任的人把某条意见聊开，而不是独自反复回放，往往能更快找回视角。", en: "Under pressure, you may become perfectionist about meaning, feeling that the work must say everything at once. Critique, even gentle critique, can linger for days, and you might withdraw to process it alone. What usually helps is naming the one thing this piece is about and letting the rest belong to future work. Talking a comment through with one trusted person, rather than replaying it privately, often restores perspective faster." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是用手去思考。你可能习惯等想法完全成熟才开始动手，但制作不仅能表达意义，也能揭示意义。可以每个月安排一个“快速周”：每天从笔记里挑一个点子，做完一件小作品，不允许修改。七件放在一起展示。你在它们之间看到的规律，可能比任何计划都更清楚地告诉你，自己的作品到底关于什么。", en: "Your growth edge is thinking through your hands. You may wait for an idea to feel fully resolved before you begin, but making can reveal meaning as well as express it. Try a monthly “fast week”: one finished small piece every day, chosen from your notes, with no revisions allowed. Display all seven together. The patterns you notice across them may show you what your work is about more clearly than any plan." }
    },
    INFP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你是由内向外工作的。你的项目通常从某样你深深在乎的东西开始：一段记忆、一种价值、一个人，或一种挥之不去的感受。你常常让平凡的事物带上温柔而私人的情绪，速写本读起来也许像日记。你的作品往往显得真诚而不做作，即便细节完全属于你，观众也可能在其中认出自己的经历。", en: "As a maker, you work from the inside out. Your projects usually begin with something you care about deeply: a memory, a value, a person, a feeling you cannot quite shake. You often give ordinary things a tender, personal charge, and your sketchbooks may read like diaries. Your work tends to feel honest rather than performed, and viewers may recognise their own experience in it, even when the details are entirely yours." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你常用一个安静的问题检验每个选项：这样感觉真实吗？你可能会想出很多富有想象力、有时有点天马行空的可能，然后因为和自己的价值不合，否掉大部分。这个筛子让作品有了真诚的底色，但也可能拖慢决定。不妨写下某个选项“为什么”让你觉得不对，而不只是“不对”，这些理由往往指向作品真正需要的东西。", en: "On a project, your mind tends to test every option against a quiet question: does this feel true? You may generate many possibilities, imaginative and sometimes whimsical, then reject most because they do not fit your values. This filter gives your work integrity, but it can also slow decisions. Try writing down why an option feels wrong, not just that it does; the reasons often point to what the work really needs." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常常温暖、支持别人，也真心好奇他们的内心世界。导师可能欣赏你的真诚，同时鼓励你把选择背后的理由讲得更明白。小组项目里，如果方向和你在乎的东西冲突，你也许会变得沉默，早点说出来，能避免之后积下的委屈。观众往往会被作品的亲密感打动，尤其当你让他们可以靠近、甚至触摸它的时候。", en: "With peers, you are often warm, supportive and genuinely curious about their inner worlds. Tutors may appreciate your sincerity while encouraging you to explain your choices more explicitly. In group projects, you might go quiet if the direction conflicts with what matters to you; saying so early prevents resentment later. Audiences tend to respond to the intimacy of your work, especially when you let them get physically close to it, or even handle it." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会把对作品的批评当成对自己的评判，干脆想离这件作品远远的。被截止日期逼着妥协时，你也许觉得是在背叛最初的想法。这时比较有用的是制造一点距离：把作品拍下来，当成别人做的东西去看。选出一个这次绝不让步的价值，其他部分都允许灵活处理，也提醒自己，下一件作品还有机会。", en: "Under pressure, you may feel that a critique of the work is a judgement of who you are, and pull away from the piece entirely. Deadlines that force compromise can feel like a betrayal of the idea. What usually helps is creating a little distance: photograph the work and look at it as if someone else made it. Choose one value you will not compromise on this time, let everything else be flexible, and remember the next piece is another chance." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是让自己私密的语言被别人读懂，同时不被稀释。你的作品常常承载着你强烈感受到、却还没说出口的意义。可以为每件完成的作品写一段一百字左右的说明，读给一个没看过作品的人听，留意对方在哪里被吸引，在哪里露出困惑。修改的是文字，而不是感受，直到两者对得上。", en: "Your growth edge is making your private language legible to others without diluting it. Your work often carries meaning that you feel strongly but have not yet put into words. Try writing a hundred-word statement for each finished piece, then reading it aloud to someone who has not seen the work. Notice where they lean in and where they look puzzled. Revise the words, not the feeling, until the two match." }
    },
    ENFJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，驱动你的是作品能在人与人之间发生什么。你往往从一开始就在想象观众：谁会站在它面前，他们可能有什么感受，它又能引出怎样的对话。你的项目常常包含参与、教学或聚集。状态好的时候，你的作品能围绕一个共同的问题建立起社群，人们离开时，彼此之间比来时更亲近。", en: "As a maker, you are motivated by what work can do between people. You tend to imagine the audience from the start: who will stand in front of this, what they might feel, what conversation it could begin. Your projects often involve participation, teaching or gathering. At its best, your work builds community around a shared question, and people leave feeling more connected to each other than when they arrived." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常在“人”和“目的”之间来回。你可能先问一个群体或一个地方需要什么，再感觉出能服务于它的方向。你擅长读懂现场的气氛，并在进行中调整计划。风险在于让别人的需求决定了所有参数，所以不妨在速写本里留一页，写下“如果没有别人参与，我会做什么”。", en: "On a project, your mind often moves between people and purpose. You may start by asking what a group or place needs, then sense a direction that serves it. You are good at reading a room and adjusting the plan as it unfolds. The risk is letting other people’s needs set every parameter, so keep one page of your sketchbook for what you would make if nobody else were involved." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常常是那个注意到谁遇到困难、并为他们腾出空间的人。导师可能会依靠你来带动小组评图的气氛。合作时，你倾向于凝聚共识、维持士气，但也许会揽下超过公平份额的工作。观众通常会在你的作品里感到被欢迎。也请记得，用善意处理的分歧，可以是一份礼物，而不是对和谐的威胁。", en: "With peers, you are often the person who notices who is struggling and makes space for them. Tutors may rely on you to energise a group crit. In collaborations, you tend to build consensus and keep morale up, though you might absorb more of the workload than is fair. Audiences usually feel welcomed by your work. Remember that disagreement, handled kindly, can be a gift rather than a threat to harmony." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能对每个人都说“好”，然后悄悄发现留给自己作品的时间不够了。冷冰冰的评图意见，对你的影响也许比表现出来的更大，你可能会过度解释，试图修补现场的气氛。这时比较有用的是守住一段固定的独处创作时间，把它当成正式约定。艰难的评图之后，把关于作品的意见和现场的感受分开写下来。", en: "Under pressure, you may say yes to everyone, then quietly run out of time for your own work. Critique delivered coldly can affect you more than you show, and you might over-explain to repair the moment. What usually helps is protecting a fixed block of solitary studio time and treating it as an appointment. After a difficult crit, write down what was said about the work separately from how it felt in the room." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是偶尔把自己的创作声音放在中心。你为别人付出很多，作品也可能变成装别人故事的容器，而不是你自己的。可以每个学期做一个完全由自己决定、不需要向任何人解释的项目，在完成之前保持小规模、不公开。你可能会发现，它非但不会和你的合作实践竞争，反而让它更有力量。", en: "Your growth edge is putting your own creative voice at the centre now and then. You give a great deal to others, and your work can become a container for their stories rather than yours. Try one self-directed project each term that you do not need to justify to anyone. Keep it small and private until it is finished. You may find it strengthens, rather than competes with, your collaborative practice." }
    },
    ENFP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你是一台可能性的发生器。一次散步、一段对话或一部电影，都可能让你记满一整本点子，你也常常看到别人眼中毫不相干的事物之间的联系。你的作品往往俏皮、温暖、涉猎广泛，在不同媒介之间自如切换，人们会被其中的能量吸引。最好的作品，通常出现在你的热情遇上一个你真心在乎的想法时。", en: "As a maker, you are a generator of possibilities. A single walk, conversation or film can leave you with a notebook full of ideas, and you often see links between things others keep apart. Your work tends to be playful, warm and wide-ranging, moving easily between media, and people are drawn to its energy. The strongest pieces usually happen when your enthusiasm meets one idea you genuinely care about." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路往往先扩张、再收缩。你可能一口气想出几十个方向，每个都让你兴奋，而且不愿过早缩小范围。你最好的点子，常常出现在聊天中途，或者在做另一件事的时候。难点在于记下来：一面贴满便利贴的墙，或随手录语音的习惯，都能接住这些火花。然后用你的价值去挑选：哪个想法一个月后你仍愿意为它辩护？", en: "On a project, your mind tends to expand before it contracts. You may brainstorm dozens of directions, each with its own excitement, and resist narrowing too soon. Your best ideas often arrive mid-conversation or while making something else. The challenge is keeping track: a wall of sticky notes or a voice-memo habit can catch the sparks. Then choose with your values, asking which idea you would still defend in a month." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是那个让工作室气氛变轻松的鼓励者，你对别人作品的兴奋很有感染力。导师可能欣赏你的开放，同时会推着你做选择。合作时，你往往在项目开头最出色，到了重复性的制作阶段就容易泄气，和一个喜欢收尾的人搭档效果很好。观众通常会回应你的温度，尤其当作品邀请他们一起玩、一起参与时。", en: "With peers, you are often an encourager who makes a studio feel lighter, and your excitement about other people’s work is infectious. Tutors may enjoy your openness and push you to choose. In collaborations, you tend to excel at the start, then lose energy during repetitive production stages, so pairing with someone who loves finishing works well. Audiences usually respond to your warmth, especially when the work invites them to play or contribute." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会在任务之间跳来跳去，不断冒出新点子，把最难的部分拖到最后一晚。评图意见带来的刺痛也许比你表现出来的更深，因为你在每个项目里都投入了很多自己。这时比较有用的是一张只写三件事的短清单，再找一个朋友互相督促。被意见刺到时，问问自己：哪一条如果照做，会让作品更像你？", en: "Under pressure, you may jump between tasks, start fresh ideas and leave the hardest part until the last night. Critique can sting more than you show, because you invest so much of yourself in each project. What usually helps is a visible to-do list with only three items, and a friend to check in with. When a crit hurts, ask yourself which comment, if acted on, would make the work feel more like you." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是把火花变成持续的系列作品。你的很多点子，值得比它们实际得到的更多时间。可以试一条“进一出一”的规则：开始一个新项目之前，先完成或郑重归档一个旧项目，并写一句为什么。准备一个文件夹，只放你完成的项目。每个学期翻一翻，你可能会发现作品里一条自己从未留意的线索。", en: "Your growth edge is turning sparks into sustained bodies of work. Many of your ideas deserve more time than they get. Try a “one in, one out” rule: before starting a new project, finish or deliberately archive an existing one with a short note on why. Keep a single folder for the projects you finish. Looking through it each term may show you a thread running through your work that you had not noticed." }
    },
    ISTJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你习惯在坚实的地基上建造。你倾向于信任自己验证过的方法，也以把事情做到位为荣：尺寸准确、边缘干净、文件标注清楚。你的速写本可能是一份井井有条的过程记录，几个月后依然有用。你的作品常有一种安静的权威感，因为工艺经得起细看。大家会慢慢发现，你答应交付的东西，通常会完整、准时地出现。", en: "As a maker, you build on solid ground. You tend to trust methods you have tested, and you take pride in doing things properly: accurate measurements, clean edges, carefully labelled files. Your sketchbooks may be orderly records of process, still useful months later. Your work often has a quiet authority, because the craft holds up to close inspection. People learn that what you say you will deliver usually arrives, complete and on time." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常从“先例”开始。你可能会先看看类似的问题以前是怎么解决的，收集可靠的参考，再仔细比较。你倾向于一步一步推进，确认好这一阶段再进入下一阶段。这让你的过程很可靠，但也可能让大胆的偏离显得有风险。每个项目留出一个“故意做错”的小测试，能让你看到常规方法之外有什么。", en: "On a project, your mind often begins with precedent. You may look at how similar problems were solved before, gather reliable references and compare them carefully. You tend to move step by step, checking each stage before starting the next. This makes your process dependable, though it can make radical departures feel risky. Setting aside one small test per project as “deliberately wrong” can show you what lies outside your usual method." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是那个靠得住的人：知道设备预约表放在哪里，也清楚题目真正要求什么。导师可能看重你的稳定，同时鼓励你多冒一些险。小组项目里，你倾向于守住时间线和质量标准。观众通常信任你的作品，因为它明显经过深思熟虑。偶尔把你的推理过程说出来，能让别人看见精确背后的思考。", en: "With peers, you are often the reliable one who knows where the equipment booking sheet is and what the brief actually requires. Tutors may value your consistency and encourage you to take more risks. In group projects, you tend to hold the timeline and the quality standards. Audiences usually trust your work because it is clearly considered. Sharing a little of your reasoning aloud helps others see the thinking behind the precision." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会更紧地抓住原定计划，别人临时改动时也容易沮丧。如果评图意见只是让你“放开一点”，你也许会觉得含糊、帮不上忙。这时比较有用的是把反馈转成具体行动：问导师，一个具体的改变会是什么样子。守住日常节奏，尤其是睡眠和按时吃饭，因为稳定的底盘，能让你在作品本身上更敢冒险。", en: "Under pressure, you may cling more tightly to your plan and become frustrated when others change it at the last minute. A crit that simply asks you to loosen up can feel vague and unhelpful. What usually helps is turning feedback into a concrete action: ask the tutor what one specific change would look like. Keep your routine, especially sleep and regular meals, because a stable base lets you take risks in the work itself." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是“有控制的实验”。你不必放弃方法，而是可以把实验也变成方法的一部分。可以每个月做一次“变量测试”：改变一件你一向保持不变的东西，比如尺寸、纸张、颜色或工具，做三个版本，并像记录其他事情一样认真记录结果。久而久之，这些测试可能会拓宽你的语汇，同时保留让作品值得信赖的严谨。", en: "Your growth edge is controlled experiment. You do not need to abandon method; you can make experiment part of it. Try a monthly “variable test”: change one thing you always keep the same, such as scale, paper, colour or tool, and make three versions. Record the results as carefully as you record everything else. Over time, these tests may widen your vocabulary while keeping the rigour that makes your work trustworthy." }
    },
    ISFJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你会留意别人忽略的小事：一页纸的分量，一道收住布边的针脚，以及将来会使用这件作品的人。你常常从记忆、家庭和日常的小仪式中汲取素材。你的作品往往耐心而精致，带着一种观众在说清之前就能感受到的关怀。安静之中，你可能是工作室里手上功夫最好的人之一。", en: "As a maker, you are attentive to the small things others overlook: the weight of a page, the stitch that holds a seam, the person who will use what you make. You often draw on memory, family and everyday rituals. Your work tends to be patient and finely crafted, carrying a sense of care that viewers feel before they can explain it. Quietly, you may be one of the most skilled pairs of hands in the studio." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常依托积累下来的经验。你可能清楚记得某种材料上次是怎么反应的，并用这份经验避开错误。你倾向于精修而不是推倒重来，靠许多细小的调整让作品变好。这会带来漂亮的完成度，但也可能让你一直停在熟悉的领域。问问自己“一个我欣赏的人，在这里会怎么做得不一样”，可能会打开一条新路。", en: "On a project, your mind often works through accumulated experience. You may remember exactly how a material behaved last time and use that knowledge to avoid mistakes. You tend to refine rather than reinvent, improving a piece through many small adjustments. This yields a beautiful finish, though it may keep you close to familiar ground. Asking “what would someone I admire do differently here?” can open a new route." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是那个记得别人生日、愿意借工具、也会注意到谁快撑不住的人。导师可能看到你的勤奋，也想听到更多你的观点。小组项目里，你倾向于接下细致却不起眼的工作，这值得被看见和肯定。观众常常在你的作品里感到被照顾。评图时，不妨多说几次“我认为”，你的看法值得被听到。", en: "With peers, you are often the one who remembers birthdays, lends tools and notices when someone is overwhelmed. Tutors may see your diligence and want to hear more of your opinion. In group projects, you tend to take on the careful, unglamorous tasks, which deserve credit. Audiences often feel looked after by your work. Let yourself say “I think” more often in crits; your view is worth hearing." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会替别人分担额外的工作，自己的项目却落后了，然后在心里默默委屈。如果评图意见触及与家庭或记忆相关的部分，你也许会觉得是在针对个人。这时比较有用的是在答应帮忙之前，先写下自己的优先事项清单。艰难的评图之后，翻翻你的过程笔记，它们会提醒你已经解决了多少问题，下一步又在哪里。", en: "Under pressure, you may take on extra work for others while your own project slips, then feel quietly resentful. Critique can feel personal if it touches something tied to family or memory. What usually helps is a clear list of your own priorities, written before you agree to help anyone. After a hard crit, revisit your process notes: they remind you how much you have already solved, and where the next step lies." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是对自己的判断更有信心。你懂的往往比你说出来的多，你对工艺和意义的看法也很有根据。可以在每页速写本末尾写一句“我认为这样可行，因为……”，每个月在评图时把其中一句大声说出来。慢慢地，表达观点可能会变得容易，你的作品也可能因此变得更大胆。", en: "Your growth edge is confidence in your own judgement. You often know more than you say, and your opinions on craft and meaning are well founded. Try ending each sketchbook page with one sentence beginning “I believe this works because…”, and once a month, share one of those sentences aloud in a crit. Over time, stating your view may become easier, and your work may grow bolder as a result." }
    },
    ESTJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你是能把事情做成的人。你倾向于把一份题目看成一项要按时、按标准做好的任务，并享受作品完成、真正能用时的满足感。你的作品集可能展现出务实的智慧：每个项目都经过计划、测试和交付。大家放心把统筹和制作交给你。状态好的时候，你的作品兼具清晰的目的和很高的完成度。", en: "As a maker, you get things made. You tend to see a brief as a job to be done well, on time and to standard, and you enjoy the satisfaction of a finished, functioning result. Your portfolio may show practical intelligence: projects that were planned, tested and delivered. People trust you with logistics and production. At its best, your work combines clarity of purpose with a high level of finish." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路倾向于“组织”。你可能先从限制条件出发，预算、时间和材料，再设计出符合这些条件的方案。你擅长比别人更早看出哪些东西在实际中行不通。风险在于把看似不切实际、但经过发展可能很有力的想法过早否掉。所以在排除之前，给每个大胆的提议一次诚实的测试。", en: "On a project, your mind tends to organise. You may start with the constraints of budget, time and materials, and design the solution to fit them. You are good at spotting what will not work in practice before anyone else does. The risk is dismissing ideas that seem impractical but could become strong with development, so give every wild suggestion one honest test before you rule it out." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是把模糊的小组计划变成时间表的那个人。导师可能欣赏你的可靠，同时推动你做更开放的探索。合作时，你倾向于清楚地提出要求，这很有帮助，只是直率的语气有时比你想的更重。观众通常会被你专业的呈现方式打动。在给出结论之前，先问一个出于好奇的问题，能让气氛缓和下来。", en: "With peers, you are often the person who turns a vague group plan into a schedule. Tutors may appreciate your reliability and push you towards more open-ended enquiry. In collaborations, you tend to set expectations clearly, which helps, although a blunt tone can land harder than you intend. Audiences usually respond to the professionalism of your presentation. Asking one curious question before giving your verdict can soften the room." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会更用力地推进，事事亲自掌控，对看起来拖慢进度的人失去耐心。如果评图意见针对的是概念而不是执行，你也许会觉得不够清楚。这时比较有用的是追问标准：“更好的版本需要什么？”在每份时间表里预留一点缓冲。出问题时也请记得，需要调整的是流程，而不是你的能力。", en: "Under pressure, you may push harder, take charge of every detail and lose patience with people who seem to be slowing things down. Critique about concept rather than execution can feel unclear. What usually helps is asking for criteria: “What would a stronger version need?” Build a small buffer into every timeline, and when things go wrong, remember that it is the process, not your competence, that needs adjusting." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是对开放问题多一些耐心。很多最有意思的作品，开始时并没有清楚的答案，不确定本身就是方法的一部分。可以为一个项目写一本“为什么”日记：每天写下一个关于题目、你暂时还答不出的问题，不急着解决。最后再从头读一遍。你可能会发现，这些问题比计划更适合作为丰富的起点。", en: "Your growth edge is patience with open questions. Some of the most interesting work starts without a clear answer, and the uncertainty is part of the method. Try keeping a “why” journal for one project: each day, write one question about the brief that you cannot yet answer, without trying to solve it. At the end, read them back. You may find the questions make a richer starting point than the plan." }
    },
    ESFJ: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你在创作时心里总装着人。你倾向于思考作品会被怎样接收、分享和记住，当一个项目把大家聚在一起时，你往往状态最好。你可能会被活动、展览、工作坊，以及为特定社群而做的作品吸引。你的作品通常让人感到慷慨、被妥帖招待，仿佛每位观众到来之前，都已经被考虑过了。", en: "As a maker, you create with people in mind. You tend to think about how work will be received, shared and remembered, and you are often at your best when a project brings others together. You may be drawn to events, exhibitions, workshops and work made for specific communities. Your work usually feels generous and well hosted, as if every visitor has been considered before they arrive." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常在“人的需要”和“实际细节”之间切换。你可能会想象观众走过一个空间，并围绕他们的舒适安排每一个环节。你倾向于借鉴类似活动、类似人群中已经被证明有效的做法。风险在于为了让每个人满意而一味遵循惯例。尽早问自己，什么能让这个项目明显属于你，并守住这一部分。", en: "On a project, your mind often moves between people’s needs and practical detail. You may picture the audience walking through a space and plan each moment around their comfort. You tend to draw on what has worked before at similar events or with similar groups. The risk is following convention to please everyone. Ask early what would make this project distinctively yours, and protect that element." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是一届学生的黏合剂：组织群聊、张罗庆祝、整理共享资源。导师可能很看重你帮忙营造的氛围。合作时，你倾向于让每个人都被纳入、都知情，但也许会回避一些必要的冲突。观众通常会感到被真心欢迎。也请记得，对朋友的作品给出诚实、具体的反馈，同样是一种关怀。", en: "With peers, you are often the glue of a cohort, organising group chats, celebrations and shared resources. Tutors may value the atmosphere you help create. In collaborations, you tend to keep everyone included and informed, though you might avoid conflict that needs to happen. Audiences usually feel genuinely welcomed. Remember that honest, specific feedback to a friend about their work is also a form of care, and often the most useful one." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会忙着让身边每个人都满意，却忽略了自己的截止日期。冷淡或当众的批评可能让你动摇，你也许会同时向很多人寻求安慰。这时比较有用的是只选一位信任的人好好聊一聊。保持简单的日常节奏，睡前写下明天的三件事，并提醒自己：让别人短暂失望，并不等于辜负了他们。", en: "Under pressure, you may focus on keeping everyone else happy and neglect your own deadline. Critique that feels cold or public can shake you, and you might seek reassurance from many people at once. What usually helps is choosing one trusted person to talk it through with. Keep a simple routine, write tomorrow’s three tasks before bed, and remind yourself that disappointing someone briefly is not the same as letting them down." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是倾听自己真正想做的东西。你很擅长读懂别人的期待，而这可能会盖过你内心比较安静的兴趣。可以每个月独自散一次步，带上相机或速写本，只记录吸引你的东西，不考虑任何观众，并把这些页面留给自己。一年下来，它们可能会显露出一个属于你的方向，与你以人为中心的创作并行，并让它更丰富。", en: "Your growth edge is listening to your own creative wants. You are skilled at reading what others expect, which can drown out quieter personal interests. Try a monthly solo walk with a camera or sketchbook, recording only what attracts you, with no audience in mind. Keep these pages private. Over a year, they may reveal a personal direction that can sit alongside, and enrich, your people-centred work." }
    },
    ISTP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你靠上手来理解事物。你常常想知道一个机构怎么运转，一个接口为什么会坏，一种材料在高温或受力时会发生什么。你在工坊里的习惯可能高效又有创意，没有合适的工具时，你也乐意自己做一把。你的作品往往简洁、构造精良，没有为了好看而添加的东西，每个部分都在发挥作用。", en: "As a maker, you understand things by handling them. You often want to know how a mechanism works, why a joint fails, what a material does under heat or pressure. Your workshop habits may be efficient and inventive, and you are happy to build your own tools when none exist. Your work tends to be lean and well engineered, with nothing added for show and every part doing its job." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路倾向于“排查故障”。你可能先快速做出一个粗糙的版本，看哪里出问题，再分析原因。你很适应技术难题，常能找到优雅、省料的解决办法。风险在于一直停留在解决问题的状态，却从未把概念说出来。简单记录每一个决定，写下为什么选这个方案，之后就能成为作品说明的骨架。", en: "On a project, your mind tends to troubleshoot. You may start by making a rough version quickly, seeing what breaks and working out why. You are comfortable with technical problems and often find elegant, economical solutions. The risk is staying in problem-solving mode and leaving the concept unspoken. Keeping a simple log of decisions, noting why you chose each solution, can later become the backbone of your written statement." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，激光切割机卡住了、作品立不起来时，大家常常会来问你。导师可能欣赏你的动手能力，同时会轻轻推你多谈谈意义。小组里，你倾向于动手而不是开会，可能一转身就消失在工坊里，发一句简短的进度更新，能让大家安心。观众常常欣赏作品的做法，一段简短的过程说明能帮他们注意到这些细节。", en: "With peers, you are often the person people ask when the laser cutter jams or a piece will not stand up. Tutors may admire your practical skill and nudge you to talk more about meaning. In groups, you tend to prefer doing over meeting, and may disappear into the workshop, so a quick update keeps others in the loop. Audiences often appreciate how your work is made; a short process note helps them notice." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会变得沉默，一个人默默修问题，别人却不知道你在哪里。如果评图意见显得情绪化或含糊，你也许会直接“关机”。这时比较有用的是追问细节：是哪个部分，怎样会更好？身体活动，哪怕只是散步或骑一小段车，往往能让头脑清醒。时间紧时，尽早决定哪些技术上的精修可以留到交稿之后。", en: "Under pressure, you may go quiet and work alone, fixing problems in silence while others wonder where you are. Critique that feels emotional or vague can make you switch off. What usually helps is asking for specifics: which part, and what would improve it? Physical activity, even a short walk or bike ride, often clears your head. When time is tight, decide early which technical refinements can wait until after the hand-in." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方是“语言”。你的作品往往能通过制作本身清楚地表达，但评图、申请和展览也需要你开口解释。可以在每次工作室时间之后，录一段两分钟的语音，像讲给一个好奇的朋友那样，说说你做了什么、为什么这样做。每周挑一段整理成三句话。慢慢地，你可能会建立起一套和你的双手一样精确的语汇。", en: "Your growth edge is language. Your work often speaks clearly through its making, but crits, applications and exhibitions also ask you to explain it. Try recording a two-minute voice memo after each studio session, describing what you did and why, as if to a curious friend. Transcribe one each week and edit it down to three sentences. Over time, you may build a vocabulary that matches the precision of your hands." }
    },
    ISFP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你首先用感官回应世界：笔刷拖过的阻力，釉面的光泽，午后光线让一种颜色发生的变化。你倾向于跟随“感觉对”的东西，而不是流行的东西，速写本里也许满是色样、肌理和小习作。你的作品常带着一种难以伪装的个人情绪，邀请观众慢下来去感受，而不是去解码。", en: "As a maker, you respond first through the senses: the drag of a brush, the sheen of a glaze, the way afternoon light changes a colour. You tend to follow what feels right rather than what is fashionable, and your sketchbooks may be full of swatches, textures and small studies. Your work often carries a personal mood that is hard to fake, inviting viewers to slow down and feel rather than decode." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常在当下的制作与调整中展开。你也许不会事先规划最终画面，但一笔不对时，你立刻就知道。你的决定来自内心对“真诚”和“美”的强烈感觉。风险在于，这个过程在别人眼里几乎是看不见的。把每个阶段拍下来，再写一个词记下当时的感受，之后就能让你的思考被读懂。", en: "On a project, your mind often works through making and adjusting in the moment. You may not plan the final image, but you know immediately when a mark is wrong. Decisions come from a strong inner sense of what is honest and what is beautiful. The risk is that this process stays invisible to others. Photographing each stage and noting one word about how it felt can make your thinking legible later." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常常温和、包容，安静地鼓励别人。比起大组讨论，你可能更喜欢一对一的交谈。导师可能欣赏你的眼光，同时请你把选择讲得更完整。合作时，你倾向于随和配合，直到某件事和你的价值冲突，才会坚定地不让步。观众通常会被作品丰富的感官质感打动，尤其是亲眼看到实物的时候。", en: "With peers, you are often gentle, accepting and quietly encouraging, and you may prefer one-to-one conversations to large group discussions. Tutors may admire your eye and ask you to explain your choices more fully. In collaborations, you tend to go along easily until something conflicts with your values, then you dig in firmly. Audiences usually respond to the sensory richness of your work, especially when they can see it in person." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会卡住，甚至干脆回避作品，尤其当截止日期要求你走一个自己没感觉的方向时。批评也许会让你觉得是在否定你的品位，而品位对你来说非常私人。这时比较有用的是回到材料里：花一小时不设目标地动手做，往往能让你重新站稳。之后，从反馈中挑出和你直觉相符的那一条，从那里开始。", en: "Under pressure, you may freeze or avoid the work altogether, especially if the deadline demands a direction you do not feel. Critique can seem like a judgement on your taste, which is deeply personal. What usually helps is returning to the material: an hour of hands-on making with no goal often restores your footing. Afterwards, choose the one piece of feedback that matches your own instinct and start there." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方，是给作品配上文字。你的图像和物件本身会说话，但一段清楚的文字背景，能让更多人跟上你的方向。可以给每件完成的作品写一段简短的说明：用了什么材料，因什么而起，以及你希望留住的一种感受。语言保持平实。久而久之，这些说明可能会成为一份一读就知道是你写的创作陈述的基础。", en: "Your growth edge is pairing your work with words. Your images and objects speak, but a clear written context lets more people follow where you are going. Try writing a short caption for every finished piece: what it is made of, what prompted it, and one feeling you hoped to hold. Keep the language plain. Over time, these captions may become the basis for statements that sound unmistakably like you." }
    },
    ESTP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你在动手的那一刻最有活力。你往往通过直接上手学得最快：在真实空间里测试，再根据结果即时反应。现场活动、拍摄和装置，都很契合你对时机和风险的直觉。你的作品常带着即时感和胆量，有一种人们还没开始思考就先感受到的身体存在。项目需要有人立刻行动时，你可能是第一个举手的。", en: "As a maker, you come alive in the moment of doing. You tend to learn fastest by jumping in, testing something in real space and reacting to what happens. Live events, shoots and installations suit your instinct for timing and risk. Your work often has immediacy and nerve, with a physical presence that people feel before they think. You may be the first to volunteer when a project needs someone to act." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路倾向于“实时运行”。你可能会跳过冗长的计划，问题出现时再解决，失败了也能迅速调整。你擅长读懂一个空间、一群人或一个情境，并当场做出务实的决定。风险在于，一个项目的经验还没留下，下一个就开始了。每次工作结束后花五分钟复盘，能让你的直觉慢慢沉淀成方法。", en: "On a project, your mind tends to work in real time. You may skip long planning and solve problems as they appear, adapting quickly when something fails. You are good at reading a space, a crowd or a situation and making practical decisions on the spot. The risk is that lessons from one project vanish before the next begins. A five-minute debrief after each session helps your instincts build into method." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常常精力充沛、合作起来很有趣，总是那个说“现在就试试”的人。导师可能喜欢你的胆量，同时希望你多一些反思。小组项目里，你倾向于在动手和现场环节大放异彩，到了整理记录时就容易失去兴趣。观众通常会回应你的临场感和节奏。尽早商量好由谁来写项目总结，能避免最后的摩擦。", en: "With peers, you are often energetic and fun to work with, the one who suggests trying it now rather than talking about it. Tutors may enjoy your boldness and ask for more reflection. In group projects, you tend to excel in the hands-on and live parts, and may lose interest in documentation. Audiences usually respond to your presence and timing. Agreeing early who writes up the project avoids last-minute friction." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会坐立不安，走捷径，或者把文字部分拖到最后一刻。如果评图意见一直停留在理论上，你也许会觉得离自己实际做的事很远。这时比较有用的是追问：这条意见落到实际，会怎样改变下一版？短时间的身体活动能让你迅速恢复状态。为文字任务预约一个固定的小时，并把它当成一场现场演出的开场时间。", en: "Under pressure, you may get restless, take shortcuts or leave the written parts until the last minute. Critique that dwells on theory can feel remote from what you actually did. What usually helps is asking how the comment would change the next version in practical terms. Short bursts of physical activity reset you quickly. Book a specific hour for the writing tasks, and treat that hour like the start time of a live show." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方是“反思”。你积累丰富经验的速度很快，但只有回头看，它们才会变成方法。可以为每个项目写一页记录，分三栏：发生了什么、什么有效、下次会改什么，每页配一张照片。几个月后把它们放在一起读。你可能会发现自己直觉中的规律，让下一次冒险变得更聪明，而不是更保守。", en: "Your growth edge is reflection. You gather rich experience quickly, but it becomes a method only when you look back. Try keeping a one-page log for each project with three headings: what happened, what worked, what you would change. Add one photograph per page. After a few months, read them together. You may notice patterns in your instincts that make the next risk smarter, not smaller." }
    },
    ESFP: {
      portrait: { zh: "作为创作者，你天生有一种存在感。你往往知道什么能吸引目光、点亮现场，或者让观众笑出来，也容易被色彩、动作、服装和表演吸引。你的作品常常鲜活而慷慨，适合和别人一起体验，而不是独自研究，人们会记住它。最有力量的时刻，通常是这份能量承载着你真心感受到的东西的时候。", en: "As a maker, you have a natural sense of presence. You tend to know what will catch an eye, lift a room or make an audience laugh, and you are drawn to colour, movement, clothing and performance. Your work often feels alive and generous, made to be experienced with others rather than studied alone, and people remember it. Its strongest moments usually come when that energy carries something you genuinely feel." },
      thinking: { zh: "做项目时，你的思路常常是“先试穿”，无论字面意义还是引申意义。你可能早早就搭配、拍摄或表演一个粗略版本，再凭现场的感觉判断好坏。你对别人的反应回应很快。风险在于只依靠表面吸引力继续推进，却没有追问它为什么有效。每次测试之后问一句“这到底关于什么”，能增加深度，又不拖慢你的节奏。", en: "On a project, your mind often works by trying things on, literally or figuratively. You may style, shoot or perform a rough version early and judge it by how it feels in the room, responding quickly to people’s reactions. The risk is building on surface appeal without examining why it works. Asking “what is this really about?” after each test adds depth without slowing your momentum." },
      others: { zh: "和同学相处时，你常是那个在漫长一天里让工作室重新热闹起来的温暖存在。导师可能欣赏你的表现力，同时推动你多做研究。合作时，你倾向于在镜头前或观众面前发光，也能围绕一次拍摄把大家聚起来。观众通常能直接感受到你。给比较安静的合作者也留出发光的空间，能让你的项目更有力量。", en: "With peers, you are often the warm, lively presence who lifts the studio on a long day. Tutors may enjoy your expressiveness and push for more research. In collaborations, you tend to shine in front of the camera or the audience and bring people together around a shoot. Audiences usually connect with you directly. Making space for quieter collaborators to shine too can make your projects even stronger." },
      pressure: { zh: "压力之下，你可能会寻找各种分心的事，答应社交邀约，把困难的任务拖到很晚。批评也许会让你觉得是针对个人，因为作品里能看到太多你自己。这时比较有用的是一份清晰、简短的计划，并把好玩的事当作完成后的奖励。约一个朋友一起在工作室做事。评图之后，挑一条意见马上动手改，让情绪转化为进展。", en: "Under pressure, you may seek distraction, say yes to social plans and leave difficult tasks until very late. Critique can feel personal because so much of you is visible in the work. What usually helps is a clear, short plan with something fun built in as a reward. Work alongside a friend in the studio. After a crit, pick one comment to act on immediately, so the feeling turns into progress." },
      growth: { zh: "你值得发展的地方是研究的厚度。你对感染力的直觉很强，而背景研究能让它更有持久的分量。可以为每个项目建一个小小的资料库：从历史、电影、时装或日常生活中找十个参考，每个配一句话，说明它为什么重要，钉在你的工作台旁边。慢慢地，你可能会发现作品变得更让人难忘，因为表面之下有了故事。", en: "Your growth edge is research depth. Your instinct for impact is strong, and context can give it lasting weight. Try building a small archive for each project: ten references from history, film, fashion or everyday life, each with one sentence on why it matters. Pin it beside your workspace. Over time, you may find your work becomes harder to forget, because it carries a story beneath the surface." }
    }
  };

  /* ---------------- preference narratives: reads after “Your X preference is <band>.” ---------------- */
  L.dimNarrative = {
    EI: {
      E: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你既能从人群中、也能从独处中获得能量，平衡点可能随项目而变化。遇到热闹的小组题目，你也许喜欢在工作室的大桌边把想法聊开；做个人作品时，你可能更想戴上耳机，找个安静的角落。值得留意一下，你最近最好的作品是在哪种环境里做出来的，因为你的作答显示，两种环境你都可能如鱼得水。", en: "That means you can draw energy from both company and solitude, and the balance may shift with the project. On a busy group brief, you might enjoy talking ideas through at the big studio table; on a personal piece, you may prefer headphones and a quiet corner. It is worth noticing which setting produced your best recent work, because your answers suggest you could thrive in either." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常发现，把想法说出来能帮助它成形；在状态低迷的日子里，热闹的工作室也能为你充电。你可能仍然需要一些安静时间，尤其是处理细致工作的时候。评图时，你也许能比较自如地边想边说。一个有用的习惯是讨论结束后马上记下自己说过的话，因为你最好的点子可能在对话中冒出来，也可能同样快地溜走。", en: "You often find that talking an idea through out loud helps it take shape, and a lively studio can lift your energy on a slow day. You probably still value some quiet time, especially for detailed tasks. In crits, you may be comfortable thinking on your feet. A useful habit is to jot down what you said straight after a discussion, because your best ideas may surface mid-conversation and slip away just as quickly." },
        clear: { zh: "身边的人和热闹的环境，持续地为你提供能量。你可能在对话中、在钉墙展示时，或者在工作室里走来走去时思考得最好，长时间独自工作则可能让你提不起劲。小组评图、合作题目和面向公众的项目，大概都很适合你。可以把需要独处的任务切成较短的时段，中间穿插一杯咖啡，或和同学聊上几句。", en: "Other people and lively surroundings are a consistent source of energy for you. You may do your best thinking in conversation, at pin-ups or while moving around the studio, and long stretches of solitary work might leave you flat. Group crits, collaborative briefs and public-facing projects are likely to suit you. Plan solitary tasks in shorter blocks, and break them up with a coffee or a quick chat with a peer." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "与人互动是你工作方式的核心。你大概在说出口时思考得最快，会从遇到的每个人那里收集想法，在热闹的环境中最有创造力。安静、孤立的时段，比如漫长的写作任务，可能会很消耗你，除非你能想办法让它变得有人陪伴。学习小组、共享工作室和定期的作品分享，都能让你持续前进。只是要确保反思也在发生，哪怕是有人陪着的反思。", en: "Interaction is central to how you work. You probably think fastest out loud, gather ideas from everyone you meet and feel most creative surrounded by activity. Quiet, isolated periods, such as a long writing task, may feel draining unless you find a way to make them social. Study groups, shared studios and regular show-and-tell sessions can keep you going. Just make sure some reflection happens, even if it is reflection in company." }
      },
      I: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你只是稍稍偏向通过独处来恢复能量，实际上，你可能在安静专注和热烈讨论之间切换自如。有些星期你也许想要共享工作室的热闹，有些星期则想关上门。你的作答显示的是灵活，而不是固定的需要。留意一下艰难的日子里什么最有帮助：如果嘈杂的房间让你疲惫，就守住安静时间；如果独处让你卡住，就约人聊聊。", en: "That means you lean only a little towards solitude for recharging, and in practice you may move easily between quiet focus and lively discussion. Some weeks you might want the buzz of a shared studio, others a closed door. Your answers suggest flexibility rather than a fixed need. Notice what helps on hard days: if the busy room drains you, protect quiet time; if isolation stalls you, arrange a chat." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常更喜欢先把事情想清楚再分享，经过一整天的评图或工作坊之后，安静的时间往往能让你重新集中。你也能享受小组合作，但最深入的创作，可能还是一个人完成的。讨论时，你也许发言不多，但每句话都经过斟酌。评图之前准备两三条笔记，能让你的思考在现场完整地被听到。", en: "You often prefer to think things through before sharing them, and quiet time tends to restore your focus after a busy day of crits or workshops. You can enjoy group work, but you might do your deepest making alone. In discussions, you may contribute fewer but more considered comments. Preparing two or three notes before a crit helps your thinking reach the room at full strength, rather than arriving an hour too late." },
        clear: { zh: "独处是你稳定的能量来源。你可能需要不被打扰的时间才能沉进一个项目，嘈杂的共享工作室或连轴转的小组讨论，也许很快就会让你疲惫。你的想法往往在私下成熟之后才拿出来。除非你说出来，导师可能意识不到你想了多少，所以随作品附上一段简短的书面反思，能让这个内在过程被看见。", en: "Solitude is a consistent source of energy for you. You may need uninterrupted time to sink into a project, and busy shared studios or back-to-back group sessions might tire you quickly. Your ideas often mature privately before they are shown. Tutors may not realise how much thinking you have done unless you tell them, so a short written reflection submitted alongside your work can make that inner process visible." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "安静而专注的时间，对你的工作方式至关重要。你大概在独处时思考得最好，常常一沉浸就是很久，大型小组和开放式工作室则可能让你消耗很大。这份内在的深度能孕育出出色的作品，但也意味着你可能分享得太少、太晚。不妨安排小而规律的一对一交流，这比小组场合轻松得多，同时依然能引入外部视角。", en: "Quiet, focused time is essential to how you work. You probably do your best thinking alone, often in long, absorbed sessions, and you may find large groups and open-plan studios draining. That inner depth can produce remarkable work. It also means you might share too little, too late. Consider arranging small, regular one-to-one check-ins, which may feel far easier than group settings while still bringing in an outside perspective." }
      }
    },
    SN: {
      S: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你对具体细节和整体可能性的关注大致相当。有些项目里，你可能从一种材料或一个观察到的场景开始；另一些项目里，则从一个想法或一种预感开始。这种平衡在工作室里很有价值，让你能用现实去检验概念。留意哪种起点能带你走得更远，卡住的时候，就借用另一种方式。", en: "That means you attend to concrete detail and to big-picture possibility in roughly equal measure. On some projects you may begin with a material or an observed scene; on others, with an idea or a hunch. This balance can be a real asset in the studio, letting you test concepts against reality. Notice which starting point tends to lead you further, and borrow the other approach when you feel stuck." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常信任看得见、摸得着、量得出的东西，也可能注意到别人错过的细节，比如纸张的纹理，或光线落在表面上的样子。你能处理抽象的想法，但大概更喜欢用真实的例子把它们落地。建立一个包含照片、样品和观察记录的参考资料库，可能比单纯阅读理论更能滋养你的项目。", en: "You often trust what you can see, touch and measure, and you may notice details that others miss, such as the grain of a paper or the way light falls on a surface. You can work with abstract ideas, but you probably prefer to ground them in real examples. Keeping a reference archive of photographs, samples and observations is likely to feed your projects more than reading theory alone." },
        clear: { zh: "具体的经验是你稳定的出发点。你可能通过示范和动手练习学得最好，也常常清楚记得一种材料的表现，或一个地方的样子。你的作品往往扎实而精确。要求抽象概念的题目，一开始也许会让你觉得抓不住；把它锚定在一件真实的物品或一个具体的地点上，能帮你找到入口。", en: "Concrete experience is your consistent starting point. You may learn best through demonstration and hands-on practice, and you often remember exactly how a material behaved or what a place looked like. Your work tends to be grounded and precise. Briefs that ask for abstract concepts might feel slippery at first; anchoring them in one real object or one specific site can help you find your way in." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常依赖可触摸的东西：事实、材料、观察到的细节和验证过的技法。你大概一眼就能看出质感、比例和实际中的缺陷，手上的功夫也可能格外精准。高度思辨的题目，如果显得与现实脱节，也许会让你沮丧。把它们当成一连串具体的实验，一次一种材料、一个情境，就能用你自己的方式去探索未知。", en: "You rely strongly on the tangible: facts, materials, observed detail and proven technique. You probably notice texture, proportion and practical flaws immediately, and your craft may be exceptionally precise. Highly speculative briefs could feel frustrating if they seem disconnected from reality. Treating them as a series of concrete experiments, one material or one scenario at a time, lets you explore the unknown on your own terms." }
      },
      N: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你对规律与可能性的兴趣，只比对具体细节稍多一点。你可能这个项目从一个想法出发，下一个项目则从一件捡来的物品开始。这种平衡让你能在想象与观察之间来回。当一个概念显得太模糊时，试着用材料测试把它落地；当一项技法变得例行公事时，问问它还能意味着什么。", en: "That means you are drawn to patterns and possibilities only a little more than to concrete detail. You may start one project with an idea and the next with a found object. This balance lets you move between imagining and observing. When a concept feels too vague, try grounding it in a material test; when a technique starts to feel routine, ask what else it could mean." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常越过眼前的东西，去看它可能意味着什么、可能变成什么。想法、隐喻和联系往往让你兴奋，你的速写本也许会很快从观察跳到概念。必要时你能处理细节，但那可能不是你的起点。养成每周用一个实物原型检验一个想法的习惯，能让你的想象力稳稳地落在地上。", en: "You often look past what is in front of you towards what it could mean or become. Ideas, metaphors and connections tend to excite you, and your sketchbook may move quickly from observation to concept. You can handle detail when it matters, but it may not be where you start. A habit of checking one idea against a physical prototype each week keeps your imagination well grounded." },
        clear: { zh: "规律、意义和未来的可能性，是你持续关注的方向。你可能很快抓住一份题目的概念，并享受重新诠释它，而具体的操作步骤则显得没那么有趣。你的作品中心往往有一个强有力的想法。为了让它真正落地，记得预留技术打磨的时间，或者和一个热爱制作环节、就像你热爱概念一样的人合作。", en: "Patterns, meanings and future possibilities are your consistent focus. You may grasp the concept of a brief quickly and enjoy reinterpreting it, while the practical steps feel less interesting. Your work often has a strong idea at its centre. To make sure that idea lands, build in time for technical refinement, or partner with someone who loves the making stage as much as you love the concept." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常深地生活在想法、符号和“如果……会怎样”的世界里。你大概处处都能看到联系，也可能觉得重复的技术工作很乏味。这能带来有野心、原创的概念，也可能意味着执行跟不上构想。为每个项目准备一张实际细节的小清单，比如尺寸、材料和时间节点，能让你的想象力自由驰骋，作品也依然能做得扎实。", en: "You live strongly in the world of ideas, symbols and what-ifs. You probably see connections everywhere and may find repetitive technical tasks tedious. This can lead to ambitious, original concepts. It can also mean that execution lags behind vision. Keeping a short checklist of practical details for each project, such as dimensions, materials and timings, lets your imagination run freely while the work still gets made well." }
      }
    },
    TF: {
      T: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你在逻辑和个人价值之间的权衡几乎各占一半。评图时，你可能这一周关注结构，下一周关注情感效果。这种平衡让你成为一个公正而多面的合作者。当一个决定卡住时，试着把两边都明确写下来：分析指向什么，感觉又认为什么是对的。把它们并排放着，答案往往就浮现出来了。", en: "That means you weigh logic and personal values in nearly equal measure. In a crit, you might focus on structure one week and on emotional effect the next. This balance can make you a fair and versatile collaborator. When a decision feels stuck, try naming both sides explicitly: what the analysis suggests, and what feels right. Seeing them side by side on the page often reveals the answer." },
        moderate: { zh: "在决定对一件作品有什么感受之前，你常常会先退一步分析它。你可能看重反馈里清楚的推理，也更喜欢能指出具体问题的批评。你依然在乎人的一面，但逻辑往往走在前面。给同学反馈时，先说哪里做得好，再说哪里还不行，能让你的清晰被对方按你的本意接收。", en: "You often step back and analyse a piece before deciding how you feel about it. You may value clear reasoning in feedback and prefer critique that names specific problems. You still care about the human side, but logic tends to lead. When giving feedback to peers, opening with what is working before you move on to what is not can help your clarity be received as you intend." },
        clear: { zh: "逻辑和客观标准，持续地引导着你的决定。你可能会根据作品是否达成目标来评判它，也大概欣赏直接、不加修饰的批评。这让你的作品严谨，你的反馈也真正有用，但也意味着别人可能会觉得你的坦率有些刺耳。在发表意见之前先问一句“你想听建议，还是想听我的第一反应？”，能让你的直接更容易被听进去。", en: "Logic and objective criteria consistently guide your decisions. You may judge work by whether it achieves its aims, and you probably appreciate direct, unvarnished critique. This makes your own work rigorous and your feedback genuinely useful. It also means others might experience your honesty as harsh. Asking “do you want suggestions or my first reaction?” before commenting can make your directness easier to hear." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常依赖分析、一致性和原则。你大概会就事论事地评估每一个决定，在评图时，也可能难以接受由情绪驱动的论点。这让你的作品拥有令人印象深刻的内在逻辑，但也可能意味着作品对观众的情感影响很少被审视。请一位信任的同学只描述作品带给对方的感受，能为你补上分析可能错过的一个维度。", en: "You rely strongly on analysis, consistency and principle. You likely evaluate every decision on its merits, and you may find emotionally driven arguments hard to accept in a crit. This gives your work an impressive internal logic. It may also mean that its emotional effect on viewers goes under-examined. Inviting one trusted peer to describe only how a piece makes them feel can add a dimension your analysis might miss." }
      },
      F: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你做决定时，个人价值和逻辑分析的分量几乎相当。有些项目里你可能跟随有意义的东西，另一些项目里则跟随单纯有效的东西。这种平衡能帮你做出既经过思考、又发自内心的作品。如果你犹豫不决，可以依次问两个问题：它站得住吗？它对我重要吗？两个答案都算数。", en: "That means personal values and logical analysis carry almost equal weight when you decide. In some projects you may follow what feels meaningful; in others, what simply works. This balance can help you make work that is both considered and heartfelt. If you are torn, try asking two questions in turn: does this hold together, and does it matter to me? Both answers count." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常通过追问“什么重要”来做决定，既对你自己重要，也对作品将触及的人重要。你可能很快察觉一件作品的情绪基调，也更喜欢真诚而友善的反馈。需要时你能分析，但价值往往走在前面。当评图让你觉得难受时，把针对作品的意见和对方说话的语气分开，能帮你真正用上这些意见。", en: "You often decide by asking what matters, both to you and to the people your work will reach. You may notice the emotional tone of a piece quickly and prefer feedback that is honest but kind. You can analyse when needed, yet values tend to lead. When a crit feels tough, separating the comment on the work from the tone it was delivered in can help you actually use it." },
        clear: { zh: "价值和对人的影响，持续地引导着你的选择。你可能会根据作品是否真实、是否用心对待它的题材来评判它。这让你的作品真诚、有情感上的共鸣，但也可能让你难以听进直率的批评，或者不太愿意给出批评。在每次同学互评中，练习提出一条具体而诚实的建议，能同时增强你的声音和你的人际关系。", en: "Values and human impact consistently guide your choices. You may judge work by whether it feels true and whether it treats its subject with care. This gives your work sincerity and emotional resonance. It may also make blunt critique hard to hear, or make you reluctant to give it. Practising one specific, honest suggestion in each peer crit can strengthen both your voice and your relationships." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常依赖个人价值、同理心和真诚。作品里有什么地方显得虚假，你大概马上就能察觉，你也可能非常在意自己的作品如何影响他人。这让你的创作实践有一种正直，但也可能让批评显得格外私人。建立一个小仪式，比如等一天再回应，然后把意见当作针对这件物品的笔记重读一遍，可能会有帮助。", en: "You rely strongly on personal values, empathy and authenticity. You probably sense immediately when something in a piece feels false, and you may care deeply about how your work affects others. This gives your practice integrity. It can also make critique feel very personal. A small ritual may help, such as waiting a day before responding, then rereading the comments as notes on the object rather than on you." }
      }
    },
    JP: {
      J: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你喜欢一些结构，但只比保持开放稍多一点。一个项目你可能仔细规划，另一个项目则也许一路即兴做到结束。这种灵活性能很好地帮到你。回想一下，上次交稿时哪种方式让你压力更小，就把它当作默认做法，同时把另一种留作备用，在项目需要时随时换上。", en: "That means you like some structure, but only a little more than you like keeping options open. On one project you may plan carefully; on another, you might improvise your way to the end. This flexibility can serve you well. Think back to which approach left you less stressed at the last hand-in, use it as your default, and keep the other in reserve for when a project calls for it." },
        moderate: { zh: "你常常希望有一份计划，知道项目正往哪里走。截止日期也许能激励你，关键决定一旦做出，你大概会觉得踏实许多。必要时你能灵活调整，但过于开放的阶段可能让你有点坐不住。在每个项目中途安排一个预定的“复盘节点”，能让你保持条理，同时为改变留一扇门。", en: "You often prefer to have a plan and a sense of where the project is going. Deadlines may motivate you, and you probably feel better once the key decisions are made. You can adapt when needed, but open-ended phases might make you a little restless. Building one planned “review point” midway through each project lets you stay organised while leaving a door open for change." },
        clear: { zh: "结构和收束，持续地帮助你把工作做好。你可能喜欢先规划好各个阶段，给自己定中间节点，宁愿早交也不愿晚交。导师大概会觉得你很靠得住。难点在于，当项目需要转向时，你能否保持开放。在计划里刻意安排一次“百搭牌”时段，尝试一个计划外的选项，能让你在不失控的前提下允许自己调整。", en: "Structure and closure consistently help you work well. You may like to map out stages, set your own interim deadlines and finish early rather than late. Tutors are likely to find you dependable. The challenge can be staying open when a project needs to change direction. Scheduling a deliberate “wild card” session, where you try one unplanned option, gives you permission to adapt without losing control." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常依赖计划、秩序和有始有终。你大概会做详细的时间表，事情悬而未决时就会不安。这让你在小组项目中效率极高、非常可靠，但也可能让临时改动或没有明确终点的题目显得压力很大。提醒自己：修改过的计划依然是计划，而一些最好的作品，恰恰出现在第一个决定之后。", en: "You rely strongly on plans, order and finishing what you start. You probably keep detailed schedules and feel uneasy when things are left unresolved. This makes you highly productive and reliable in group projects. It may also make late changes, or briefs with no clear endpoint, feel stressful. Remind yourself that a revised plan is still a plan, and that some of the best work emerges after the first decision." }
      },
      P: {
        slight: { zh: "这意味着你喜欢保持选项开放，只比喜欢清楚的计划稍多一点。你可能以灵活的方式开始一个项目，临近截止时再收紧，也可能反过来。这种平衡让你既能应变，又不会失去立足点。如果你发现自己总在最后赶工，就试着设一个固定的中间节点；如果觉得被框住了，就把时间表放松一点。", en: "That means you enjoy keeping options open only slightly more than you enjoy a clear plan. You may start a project flexibly and tighten up as the deadline nears, or the reverse. This balance lets you adapt without losing your footing. If you notice a pattern of late rushes, try one fixed midpoint; if you feel boxed in, loosen the schedule a little." },
        moderate: { zh: "在探索阶段，你常常更喜欢让项目保持开放，过早定下的计划可能让你觉得受限。新的信息能以令人兴奋的方式改变作品的方向，你大概也很享受这份灵活。截止日期也许在最后冲刺时最能激发你。给自己设两三个小小的检查节点，能把最后时刻的那股劲儿更均匀地分布到整个项目里。", en: "You often prefer to keep a project open while you explore, and you may find that early fixed plans feel limiting. New information can redirect your work in exciting ways, and you probably enjoy that flexibility. Deadlines might energise you most in the final stretch. Setting two or three small, self-imposed checkpoints can spread that last-minute energy more evenly across the whole project." },
        clear: { zh: "灵活和探索，持续地塑造着你的工作方式。你可能喜欢自由地收集、测试、改变方向，看到更多可能性之后才做决定。这常常带来新鲜、灵敏的作品，也可能让最后几周异常拥挤。把每个项目看作两个阶段：一个开放阶段，一个收束阶段，并在日历上清楚标出切换的那一天。", en: "Flexibility and exploration consistently shape how you work. You may like to gather, test and change course freely, deciding late once you have seen more possibilities. This often leads to fresh, responsive work. It may also lead to crowded final weeks. Treat each project as having two phases, an open one and a closing one, and mark the day you switch clearly on your calendar." },
        "very-clear": { zh: "你非常依赖随性、好奇和保持选项开放。你大概会一阵一阵地工作，追随有趣的岔路，不到必要时不愿承诺。这能带来精彩而出人意料的结果，也可能让长期项目难以收尾。找一位互相督促的伙伴，或者把一份题目拆成一连串很短、看得见的截止点，能驾驭你的能量，而不会削弱它。", en: "You rely strongly on spontaneity, curiosity and keeping your options open. You probably work in bursts, follow interesting tangents and resist committing until you must. This can generate brilliant, unexpected results. It may also make long projects difficult to bring to a close. Working with an accountability partner, or breaking a brief into very short, visible deadlines, can harness your energy without dampening it." }
      }
    }
  };

  /* ---------------- cognitive functions as dominant (leading) and auxiliary (supporting) ---------------- */
  L.functionsDeep = {
    Se: {
      dominant: { zh: "当外倾实感处于主导位置时，你会直接、即时地与世界接触。你对颜色、动态、质感和氛围有异常敏锐的觉察，也常常以行动而不是计划来回应。在工作室里，这可能表现为快速的材料实验、自信的现场表演，或在镜头后捕捉决定性瞬间的直觉。你的作品往往很有存在感。事后留出反思时间，能帮你把学到的东西留下来。", en: "When Extraverted Sensing leads, you engage with the world directly and in real time. You notice colour, movement, texture and atmosphere with unusual sharpness, and you often respond by acting rather than planning. In the studio this may show as fast material experiments, confident live performance or an instinct for the decisive moment behind a camera. Your work tends to have presence. Building in reflection afterwards helps you keep what you learn." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，外倾实感把你内在的逻辑或价值落到物质世界里。它让你对材料有感觉，也愿意用双手检验想法，作品因此不会停留在抽象层面。你可能会发现，自己在动手时思考得最好，去一趟工坊、市场或现场，就能解开卡住的项目。不妨有意识地把它当作从想法走向实物的路径。", en: "As your supporting function, Extraverted Sensing grounds your inner logic or values in the physical world. It gives you a feel for materials and a readiness to test ideas by hand, so your work does not stay abstract. You may notice that you think best while making, and that a quick trip to a workshop, market or site unlocks a stuck project. Use it deliberately as your route from thought to object." }
    },
    Si: {
      dominant: { zh: "当内倾实感处于主导位置时，你依托的是一座丰富的记忆细节仓库：某种材料怎样表现，某项技法需要什么，某个地方给人什么感觉。你倾向于信任经验，并通过细心的重复精进手艺。在工作室里，这常常意味着可靠的方法、精确的完成度，以及深厚的个人参考资料库。你的作品可能带着记忆与延续感。偶尔刻意尝试新东西，能让资料库继续生长。", en: "When Introverted Sensing leads, you build on a rich store of remembered detail: how a material behaved, what a technique required, how a place felt. You tend to trust experience and refine your craft through careful repetition. In the studio this often means reliable methods, a precise finish and a deep personal archive of references. Your work may carry a sense of memory and continuity. Occasional deliberate novelty keeps that archive growing." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，内倾实感让你的决定扎根于经验。它帮助你记住上次什么有效，认真做记录，并在项目繁忙时守住标准。你可能就是那个清楚知道该用哪种纸、步骤该按什么顺序的人。截止日期临近时依靠它，能让你稳下来，而你的主导功能则负责让更大的目标始终在视野之中。", en: "As your supporting function, Introverted Sensing gives your decisions a foundation in experience. It helps you remember what worked last time, keep careful records and maintain standards when a project gets busy. You may be the person who knows the exact paper stock or the correct order of steps. Leaning on it as deadlines approach can steady you, while your leading function keeps the bigger aim in view." }
    },
    Ne: {
      dominant: { zh: "当外倾直觉处于主导位置时，驱动你的是可能性。一个题目可以分叉出几十个点子，你也常能在相隔很远的领域、参考或媒介之间看到联系。在工作室里，这可能表现为飞快的头脑风暴、对题目俏皮的重新诠释，以及一本写满开头的速写本。你的作品往往新鲜而有创意。给这份广度配上一个清楚的筛选步骤，能让最好的想法走到完成。", en: "When Extraverted Intuition leads, you are driven by possibility. A single prompt can branch into dozens of ideas, and you often see connections between distant fields, references or media. In the studio this may show as rapid brainstorming, playful reinterpretation of briefs and a sketchbook full of beginnings. Your work tends to feel fresh and inventive. Pairing that range with a clear selection step helps the strongest ideas reach completion." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，外倾直觉在你核心的逻辑或价值周围拓宽视野。它带来替代方案、类比和意想不到的参考，让你的作品不至于变窄。你可能会发现，一次对话、一次在图书馆随手翻阅，或一张偶然看到的图片，就能引出下一步。在研究阶段有意识地使用它，自由地生成选项，再让你的主导功能决定哪个值得投入时间。", en: "As your supporting function, Extraverted Intuition widens the field around your core logic or values. It brings in alternatives, analogies and unexpected references, keeping your work from becoming narrow. You may find that a conversation, a browse along library shelves or a random image sparks the next step. Use it deliberately during the research phase, generating options freely, then let your leading function decide which one deserves your time." }
    },
    Ni: {
      dominant: { zh: "当内倾直觉处于主导位置时，你往往在能解释清楚之前，就已经感觉到一个想法要往哪里去。经过一段安静的沉浸之后，洞见常以一个完整而令人信服的画面出现。在工作室里，这可能表现为强烈的概念方向、对深层规律的敏感，以及经得起慢慢看的作品。你的项目往往很连贯。早点分享草图，能帮别人跟上你心里那个更清楚的构想。", en: "When Introverted Intuition leads, you sense where an idea is heading long before you can explain it. Insights often arrive as a single, convincing vision after a period of quiet absorption. In the studio this may show as strong conceptual direction, a feel for underlying patterns and work that rewards slow looking. Your projects tend to be coherent. Sharing early sketches helps others follow a vision that may be clearer to you than to them." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，内倾直觉为你的行动或关怀提供方向感。它帮助你看到一个项目可能通往哪里，读出深层的规律，并选择最有长远潜力的选项。你可能会注意到，自己偶尔会突然清楚一件作品真正关于什么。用短暂的安静休息为这些时刻留出空间，再让你的主导功能把洞见转成实际的步骤。", en: "As your supporting function, Introverted Intuition gives your action or care a sense of direction. It helps you see where a project could lead, read underlying patterns and choose the option with the most long-term potential. You may notice sudden moments of clarity about what a piece is really about. Make space for them with short, quiet breaks, then let your leading function turn that insight into practical steps." }
    },
    Te: {
      dominant: { zh: "当外倾思考处于主导位置时，你会为了取得结果去组织外部世界。你倾向于设定清楚的目标，高效地安排任务，并根据成果衡量进度。在工作室里，这可能表现为出色的项目管理、果断的取舍，以及按时交付有野心作品的能力。你的作品通常目的清晰。时常关心合作者和观众的感受，能让这份效率保持人情味。", en: "When Extraverted Thinking leads, you organise the world around you to get results. You tend to set clear goals, structure tasks efficiently and measure progress against outcomes. In the studio this may show as strong project management, decisive editing and a talent for delivering ambitious work on time. Your work usually has clarity of purpose. Checking in regularly with how collaborators and audiences feel keeps that efficiency humane." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，外倾思考把你内在的构想或经验转化为可执行的计划。它帮助你设定截止点、分配时间，并判断一件作品是否达到了它的目标。完成大型项目时，你可能会依靠它冲过终点线。尽早用它勾勒一份简单的时间表，能让你的主导功能放心地去探索，而不必担心最后来不及。", en: "As your supporting function, Extraverted Thinking turns your inner vision or experience into a workable plan. It helps you set deadlines, allocate time and judge whether a piece is achieving what it set out to do. You may rely on it to get large projects over the line. Using it early to sketch a simple schedule frees your leading function to explore with confidence, without worrying about the final week." }
    },
    Ti: {
      dominant: { zh: "当内倾思考处于主导位置时，你追寻的是事物内在的逻辑。你想从第一性原理出发弄懂一个想法或机制如何运作，也常常自己搭建框架，而不是借用现成的。在工作室里，这可能表现为精准的问题解决、富有创意的技术工作，以及经得起推敲的概念。你的作品往往很严谨。把推理过程说出来，能帮助别人欣赏它。", en: "When Introverted Thinking leads, you seek the inner logic of things. You want to understand how an idea or mechanism works from first principles, and you often build your own frameworks rather than borrowing them. In the studio this may show as precise problem-solving, inventive technical work and concepts that hold together under scrutiny. Your work tends to be rigorous. Explaining your reasoning aloud helps others appreciate it." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，内倾思考为你的能量和实验加上敏锐的分析力。它帮助你发现不一致的地方，弄清某样东西为什么有效，并加以改进。你可能会用它诊断一张失败的印刷，或一个站不住的论点。在忙碌的制作阶段之后依靠它去分析和删减，能把鲜活的原始素材变成一件专注、有理有据的最终作品。", en: "As your supporting function, Introverted Thinking gives your energy and experiments a sharp analytical edge. It helps you notice inconsistencies, understand why something worked and refine it. You may use it to diagnose a failed print or a weak argument. Leaning on it after a busy making phase, to analyse and edit, can turn lively raw material into a focused, well-reasoned final piece." }
    },
    Fe: {
      dominant: { zh: "当外倾情感处于主导位置时，你对人和人与人之间的氛围格外敏感。你常常能感觉到一个群体需要什么，观众会如何反应，以及怎样把大家聚在一起。在工作室里，这可能表现为天然的引导能力、慷慨的合作，以及为参与或共同意义而设计的作品。你的项目往往能连接人。定期为自己的创作偏好留出时间，能让这份关怀保持平衡。", en: "When Extraverted Feeling leads, you are attuned to people and the atmosphere between them. You often sense what a group needs, how an audience will respond and how to bring others together. In the studio this may show as natural facilitation, generous collaboration and work designed for participation or shared meaning. Your projects tend to connect people. Making regular time for your own creative preferences keeps that care balanced." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，外倾情感把你的构想或经验与他人连接起来。它帮助你考虑作品会被怎样接收，温暖地传达想法，并让合作者站在你这一边。你可能会用它读懂评图现场的气氛，或为特定观众设计项目的呈现方式。展示作品时借助它，能让即使复杂或技术性的作品，也显得亲切而切题。", en: "As your supporting function, Extraverted Feeling links your vision or experience to other people. It helps you consider how work will be received, communicate ideas warmly and keep collaborators on side. You may use it to read the mood of a crit or to frame a project for a specific audience. Drawing on it when you present can make even complex or technical work feel welcoming and relevant." }
    },
    Fi: {
      dominant: { zh: "当内倾情感处于主导位置时，引导你的是一枚由价值与真诚构成的内在指南针。某样东西是否真实，你常常立刻就知道，也可能拒绝去做自己不相信的作品。在工作室里，这可能表现为非常私人的题材、情感上的坦诚，以及鲜明的个人声音。你的作品往往显得真诚。为这些内在价值找到语言，能帮助别人走近你。", en: "When Introverted Feeling leads, you are guided by a strong inner compass of values and authenticity. You often know immediately whether something feels true, and you may refuse to make work you do not believe in. In the studio this may show as deeply personal subject matter, emotional honesty and a distinctive voice. Your work tends to feel sincere. Finding words for those inner values helps others meet you halfway." },
      auxiliary: { zh: "作为辅助功能，内倾情感为你的探索或行动提供一个个人的锚点。它帮助你选出对自己真正重要的想法，在潮流或期待把你拉向别处时，让作品保持真诚。你可能会把它感受为一种安静的“对”或“不对”。在多个选项之间做选择时听听它的声音，能确保你完成的作品真正反映你在乎的东西。", en: "As your supporting function, Introverted Feeling gives your exploration or action a personal anchor. It helps you choose which ideas matter to you, and keeps your work honest when trends or expectations pull elsewhere. You may notice it as a quiet sense of rightness or unease. Consulting it when you select between options helps make sure that what you finish genuinely reflects what you care about." }
    }
  };

  /* ---------------- creative paths in depth: index i matches careers[TYPE].paths[i] ---------------- */
  L.pathsDeep = {
    INTJ: [
      { zh: "想成为研究型艺术家，可以留意理论研讨扎实、允许自主项目的纯艺术课程，或欢迎研究的跨学科专业。值得培养的能力包括档案研究、学术写作、以绘画作为思考工具，以及记录一项长期探究。这个月，选一个你愿意追问一整年的问题，写在卡片上，再建一个专门的研究文件夹，放进五份资料和五个小小的视觉回应。", en: "To grow as a research-led artist, look for fine art courses with strong theory seminars and room for self-directed projects, or interdisciplinary programmes that welcome research. Useful skills include archival research, academic writing, drawing as a way of thinking and documenting a long-term enquiry. This month, choose one question you could follow for a year, write it on a card, and start a dedicated research file with five sources and five small visual responses." },
      { zh: "思辨设计通常属于关注批判性设计、交互设计、未来研究的设计类专业。值得培养的能力包括情景写作、用纸板或代码快速做原型、视觉叙事，以及阅读科技与社会相关的文字。这个月，挑一个你在日常生活中注意到的趋势，想象它二十年后的后果，从那个未来做一件小物品或一张海报，并配一段说明文字。", en: "Speculative design often sits within design courses focused on critical or interaction design, futures thinking and research. Skills worth building include scenario writing, quick prototyping in cardboard or code, visual storytelling and reading about technology and society. This month, pick a trend you notice in daily life, imagine its consequences twenty years ahead, and make one small object or poster from that future, with a caption explaining it." },
      { zh: "展陈设计融合了空间设计、室内建筑和策展研究，所以可以留意把三维思考与观众动线理解结合起来的课程。值得培养的能力包括模型制作、技术制图、照明基础和观众研究。这个月去看一场展览，画出你走过的路线平面图，标出你在哪里停留、为什么停留，再在纸上重新设计其中一个展厅，让动线更顺畅。", en: "Exhibition design draws on spatial design, interior architecture and curatorial studies, so look for courses that combine three-dimensional thinking with an understanding of how audiences move. Useful skills include model-making, technical drawing, lighting basics and visitor research. This month, visit an exhibition and sketch a plan of the route you took, marking where you paused and why. Then redesign one room on paper to improve the flow." }
    ],
    INTP: [
      { zh: "创意技术常在创意编程、交互设计或数字媒体类课程中学习，那里是代码与制作相遇的地方。值得培养的能力包括一门入门友好的编程语言、基础电子、物理计算，以及清楚记录实验的习惯。这个月，从速写本里挑一个奇怪的点子，做出你能做到的最简单可运行版本，比如一个触发声音的传感器，拍下来，再写三行说明。", en: "Creative technology is often studied through courses in creative computing, interaction design or digital media, where coding and making meet. Skills worth building include a beginner-friendly programming language, basic electronics, physical computing and clear documentation of experiments. This month, take one strange idea from your sketchbook and build the simplest working version you can, perhaps a sensor that triggers a sound, then film it and write three lines explaining it." },
      { zh: "观念创作在重视批判理论、讨论和媒介选择自由的纯艺术课程中最能成长。值得培养的能力包括细读、撰写创作陈述、跨材料工作，以及用最经济的方式呈现一个想法。这个月，写下一个人们很少质疑的预设，比如“什么才算礼物”，用三种不同的媒介做三件作品去检验它，然后给一位朋友看，听听他们先注意到什么。", en: "Conceptual practice thrives in fine art courses that value critical theory, discussion and a wide choice of media. Skills worth building include close reading, writing artist statements, working across materials and presenting an idea economically. This month, write down one assumption people rarely question, such as what counts as a gift, and make three works that each test it in a different medium. Show them to one friend and listen to what they notice first." },
      { zh: "设计研究出现在服务设计、设计人类学和用户体验等课程中，在那里，观察人与动手制作同样重要。值得培养的能力包括访谈、观察笔记、整理发现的图表和撰写清晰的报告。这个月，选一件日常物品或一个空间，比如公交站台，观察十个人怎样使用它，记下他们的行为，再把笔记整理成配有图示的一页总结。", en: "Design research appears in courses on service design, design anthropology and user experience, where observing people matters as much as making. Useful skills include interviewing, taking observation notes, mapping findings and writing clear reports. This month, choose an everyday object or space, such as a bus shelter, watch how ten people use it, note what they do, and turn your notes into a one-page summary with a diagram." }
    ],
    ENTJ: [
      { zh: "艺术指导通常从强调概念与团队合作的平面设计、传达设计、摄影或广告类课程中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括视觉研究、情绪板、字体排印、向合作者讲清需求，以及有说服力地展示一个方向。这个月，为一期杂志虚构一份题目，做一份三页的视觉方向文件，请一位同学按你的要求拍摄或绘制其中一页，留意你不得不解释清楚的地方。", en: "Art direction grows from graphic design, communication design, photography or advertising courses that emphasise concept and teamwork. Skills worth building include visual research, mood boards, typography, briefing collaborators and presenting a direction persuasively. This month, invent a brief for a magazine issue or campaign, create a three-page visual direction document, and ask a peer to photograph or illustrate one page to your brief. Notice what you had to clarify." },
      { zh: "展览制作连接着策展研究、艺术管理和空间设计。可以留意包含真实项目、预算管理和真实场地合作的课程。值得培养的能力包括排期、预算、风险规划、清楚的邮件写作，以及协调艺术家和技术人员。这个月，主动帮忙制作一场小展，哪怕只是走廊里的快闪展，并从最初的想法一直到开幕之夜，完整记录一份时间线和预算表。", en: "Exhibition production connects curatorial studies, arts management and spatial design. Look for courses that include live projects, budgets and work with real venues. Useful skills include scheduling, budgeting, risk planning, clear email writing and coordinating artists and technicians. This month, offer to help produce a small show, even a pop-up in a corridor, and keep a timeline and budget sheet from the first idea right through to opening night." },
      { zh: "创办工作室需要某个创意专业加上商业思维，所以可以留意设有职业实践模块、创业支持或行业项目的课程。值得培养的能力包括为作品定价、撰写提案、管理客户、基础财务，以及搭建作品集网站。这个月，为一个想象中的工作室画一页规划：名字、提供什么服务、三个理想客户，以及一个你现在就能真正开始的第一个项目。", en: "Founding a studio draws on a creative discipline plus business thinking, so look for courses with professional practice modules, enterprise support or industry projects. Skills worth building include pricing your work, writing proposals, managing clients, basic bookkeeping and building a portfolio website. This month, sketch a one-page plan for an imaginary studio: its name, what it offers, three ideal clients and one first project you could actually start now." }
    ],
    ENTP: [
      { zh: "评论写作通常从艺术史、视觉文化、策展，或写作分量很重的纯艺术课程中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括细致观看、搭建论点、按字数编辑、研究和采访。这个月去看一场展览，写一篇立场鲜明、五百字左右的展评，再把它删到两百字，同时不丢掉论点。然后拿给一个和你意见不同的人看，听听对方的反驳。", en: "Criticism grows from art history, visual culture, curating or fine art courses with a strong writing component. Skills worth building include close looking, structuring an argument, editing to a word count, research and interviewing. This month, visit one exhibition and write a five-hundred-word review that takes a clear position, then cut it to two hundred words without losing the argument. Share it with someone who disagrees with you, and hear them out." },
      { zh: "跨学科创作适合在允许跨媒介流动的纯艺术课程，或把艺术与科学、科技、人文结合的联合专业中发展。值得培养的能力包括快速原型、阅读本专业以外的内容、与专业人士合作，以及清楚地解释其中的联系。这个月，从科学杂志和时装或音乐杂志里各挑一篇文章，做一件真正需要两者才能成立的小作品。", en: "Interdisciplinary practice is supported by fine art courses that allow movement between media, or by joint programmes linking art with science, technology or the humanities. Useful skills include rapid prototyping, reading outside your field, collaborating with specialists and explaining connections clearly. This month, pick one article from a science magazine and one from a fashion or music magazine, and make a small piece that genuinely needs both to make sense." },
      { zh: "游戏设计可以在游戏、交互设计或数字媒体类课程中学习，不过桌游和玩法设计在任何地方都能开始。值得培养的能力包括规则撰写、试玩测试、系统思维、基础脚本和叙事设计。这个月，围绕一个你在乎的问题设计一款纸上游戏，和三位朋友玩一玩，记下他们在哪里困惑、在哪里无聊，然后修改一条规则，再玩一次。", en: "Game design is taught through courses in games, interaction design or digital media, though board games and play can begin anywhere. Skills worth building include rule writing, playtesting, systems thinking, basic scripting and narrative design. This month, design a paper game around a question you care about, play it with three friends, note where they got confused or bored, then change one rule and play it again to see what shifts." }
    ],
    INFJ: [
      { zh: "策展融合了艺术史、策展研究和纯艺术。可以留意提供展览项目、写作机会和与在职艺术家接触的课程。值得培养的能力包括研究、阐释性写作、挑选与编排作品，以及与艺术家建立信任。这个月，选一个对你重要的主题，从线上馆藏中挑出六件与它对话的作品，为你想象中的展览写一段简短的前言。", en: "Curating draws on art history, curatorial studies and fine art. Look for courses that offer exhibition projects, writing opportunities and contact with working artists. Skills worth building include research, interpretive writing, selecting and sequencing works, and building trust with artists. This month, choose a theme that matters to you, select six artworks from online collections that speak to it, and write a short introduction for your imaginary show." },
      { zh: "档案摄影适合重视研究、历史和印刷影像的摄影或纯艺术课程。值得培养的能力包括胶片与数码摄影、扫描、档案处理、图像编排，以及使用家族资料时取得知情同意。这个月，征得一位亲人的同意，扫描五张老照片，如果可以，再去照片里的地方重新拍摄，把旧照与新照配成一组小小的序列。", en: "Archive-based photography fits photography and fine art courses that value research, history and the printed image. Skills worth building include analogue and digital photography, scanning, archival handling, sequencing images and seeking consent when working with family material. This month, ask a relative for permission to scan five old photographs, rephotograph the places they show if you can, and pair old with new in a small sequence." },
      { zh: "口述史创作横跨纯艺术、社会实践、声音和纪录类课程。可以留意会讨论伦理与社群合作的专业。值得培养的能力包括访谈、录音与剪辑、转写，以及把讲述转化为作品形式。这个月，在征得同意后，和一位你认识的长辈聊二十分钟，谈谈他们记忆中的一个地方，然后挑出其中一句话，围绕它做一件小作品。", en: "Oral-history practice sits across fine art, social practice, sound and documentary courses. Look for programmes that discuss ethics and community collaboration. Skills worth building include interviewing, sound recording and editing, transcription, and turning testimony into form. This month, with their consent, record a twenty-minute conversation with an older person you know about a place they remember, then choose one sentence and make a small work around it." }
    ],
    INFP: [
      { zh: "绘本创作适合叙事方向扎实的插画课程，以及儿童读物或视觉叙事类模块。值得培养的能力包括角色设计、分镜、精炼的文字写作、翻页节奏，以及制作绘本样书。这个月，写一个关于某种细微感受、不超过三百字的故事，把它分成十二个跨页，然后一口气为每一页画出粗略的小草图。", en: "Picture-book work fits illustration courses with a strong narrative strand, as well as children’s book or visual storytelling modules. Skills worth building include character design, storyboarding, writing concise text, page pacing and preparing a book dummy. This month, write a story of under three hundred words about a small feeling, divide it into twelve spreads, and make rough thumbnails for every page in a single sitting." },
      { zh: "艺术家书介于纯艺术、插画、版画和平面设计之间，所以可以留意版画和装帧设备完善的课程。值得培养的能力包括简易装订、孔版或丝网印刷、内容编排、纸张选择，以及手写或字体。这个月，拿一首你写过的诗或一篇日记，做一本八页的手工装订小书，想一想每一次翻页如何改变阅读的感受。", en: "Artist’s books sit between fine art, illustration, printmaking and graphic design, so look for courses with good print and bookbinding facilities. Skills worth building include simple binding, risograph or screen printing, sequencing, paper choice and handwriting or type. This month, take a poem or diary entry you have written and make a small hand-bound book of eight pages, considering how each turn of the page changes the reading." },
      { zh: "独立动画可以在动画、插画和动态影像类课程中学习，可以留意鼓励个人短片创作的专业。值得培养的能力包括连续绘画、节奏掌控、分镜、声音设计，以及一款免费或价格可负担的动画软件。这个月，用不超过二十张画，为日常生活中的一个小细节做一段五秒的循环动画，比如杯子里升起的热气。", en: "Independent animation is taught in animation, illustration and moving-image courses; look for ones that encourage personal short films. Skills worth building include drawing in sequence, timing, storyboarding, sound design and confidence with a free or affordable animation program. This month, animate a five-second loop of something small from your daily life, such as steam rising from a cup, using no more than twenty drawings." }
    ],
    ENFJ: [
      { zh: "社区艺术实践通常从纯艺术、社会实践和参与式艺术类课程中成长起来，可以留意与校园之外有合作关系的专业。值得培养的能力包括工作坊引导、知情同意与安全保护的基础、共同设计、记录与评估。这个月，围绕一个共同的问题，和一个小群体，比如家人或邻居，进行一小时的共同创作，记录过程，而不只是结果。", en: "Community-engaged practice grows from fine art, social practice and participatory arts courses; look for programmes with partnerships beyond the campus. Skills worth building include workshop facilitation, the basics of consent and safeguarding, co-design, documentation and evaluation. This month, run a one-hour making session with a small group, perhaps family or neighbours, around a shared question, and photograph the process rather than only the results." },
      { zh: "艺术教育可以接在任何创意专业之后，往往还需要后续的教师培训，很多课程也设有教育或外展模块。值得培养的能力包括规划课程、清楚讲解技法、适应不同年龄，以及反思什么方法有效。这个月，设计一个四十五分钟的工作坊，教一项你熟悉的技法，找两三位更小的学习者试一试，再写下你会改进的地方。", en: "Arts education can follow any creative degree, often with teacher training later, and many courses offer education or outreach modules. Skills worth building include planning sessions, explaining techniques clearly, adapting to different ages and reflecting on what worked. This month, design a forty-five-minute workshop that teaches one technique you know well, test it with two or three younger learners, and write down what you would change." },
      { zh: "公共项目策划融合了策展研究、艺术管理和教育。可以留意在美术馆或艺术节有真实项目的课程。值得培养的能力包括活动策划、撰写活动介绍、主持讲座、建立合作关系，以及收集观众反馈。这个月，挑一场你欣赏的展览，为它设计三场配套活动，比如一场讲座、一个工作坊和一场夜间放映，每场配一个标题和一句介绍。", en: "Public programming combines curatorial studies, arts management and education. Look for courses with live projects in galleries or festivals. Skills worth building include event planning, writing listings, hosting talks, building partnerships and gathering audience feedback. This month, pick an exhibition you admire and design three events to accompany it, such as a talk, a workshop and an evening screening, each with a title and a one-line description." }
    ],
    ENFP: [
      { zh: "跨媒介叙事适合允许混合媒介的传达设计、动态影像、交互或插画类课程。值得培养的能力包括脚本写作、简单的视频剪辑、录音、网页或交互基础，以及根据不同形式改编同一个故事。这个月，选一个简短的个人小故事，用三种方式讲出来：一页漫画、一段一分钟的视频和一段音频。问问朋友，哪个版本最打动他们。", en: "Multimedia storytelling suits courses in communication design, moving image, interaction or illustration that let you mix media. Skills worth building include scripting, simple video editing, sound recording, web or interactive basics and adapting a story to each format. This month, choose a short personal anecdote and tell it three ways: as a comic strip, a one-minute video and an audio clip. Ask a friend which version moved them most." },
      { zh: "创意策划通常从带有真实客户题目的传达设计、广告、品牌或设计管理类课程中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括受众研究、撰写精炼的提案、概念开发、演示，以及在团队限制条件内工作。这个月，挑一家你喜欢的本地机构，比如一家图书馆或咖啡馆，为它构思三个宣传概念，每个用一张图和一句话概括。", en: "Creative strategy grows from communication design, advertising, branding or design management courses with live client briefs. Skills worth building include audience research, writing concise pitches, concept development, presenting and working within a team’s constraints. This month, pick a local organisation you like, such as a library or café, and develop three campaign concepts for it, each summarised in one image and one sentence, then test them on a friend." },
      { zh: "体验设计横跨交互、空间、沉浸式和服务设计类课程，可以留意鼓励与真实的人一起做原型的专业。值得培养的能力包括旅程地图、场景与灯光基础、声音、简单的交互技术和用户测试。这个月，把家里的一个房间变成一段为一位客人准备的五分钟体验，有开头、中段和结尾，然后问问对方记住了什么。", en: "Experience design sits across interaction, spatial, immersive and service design courses; look for ones that encourage prototyping with real people. Skills worth building include journey mapping, set and lighting basics, sound, simple interactive technology and user testing. This month, turn a room at home into a five-minute experience for one guest, with a beginning, a middle and an ending, then ask them what they remember." }
    ],
    ISTJ: [
      { zh: "文物修复通常需要艺术史、化学和动手工艺的扎实基础，之后往往还要进修专门的研究生课程。值得培养的能力包括细致观察、技术制图、撰写状况报告、材料科学，以及对重复性工作的耐心。这个月，挑一件家里的旧物，写一份详细的状况报告，描述它的材料、损伤和来历，并从六个固定角度为它拍照。", en: "Conservation usually requires a strong foundation in art history, chemistry and hands-on craft, often followed by specialist postgraduate study. Skills worth building include careful observation, technical drawing, condition reporting, materials science and patience with repetitive work. This month, choose an old object at home, write a detailed condition report describing its materials, damage and history, and photograph it from six consistent angles." },
      { zh: "典藏研究融合了艺术史、博物馆学和档案学。可以留意提供实物操作或藏品机构实习的课程。值得培养的能力包括编目、来源研究、使用数据库、严谨的引用，以及撰写清楚的展品说明。这个月，在本地博物馆或线上馆藏中挑一件藏品，追查你能找到的关于它来历的一切，再写一段两百字左右的展签。", en: "Collections research draws on art history, museum studies and archival studies. Look for courses that offer object handling or placements with collections. Skills worth building include cataloguing, provenance research, database use, careful citation and writing clear object labels. This month, choose one object in a local museum or an online collection, trace everything you can find about its origins, and write a two-hundred-word label for it." },
      { zh: "字体设计通常从平面设计和字体排印类课程中成长起来，之后有时还会进修专门的研究生课程。值得培养的能力包括手写字母、理解比例与间距、字体软件、历史研究和耐心的迭代。这个月，用统一的风格画出一个对你重要的单词，反复调整三次字间距，并记下你对笔画粗细和曲线做出的每一个决定。", en: "Type design grows from graphic design and typography courses, sometimes followed by specialist postgraduate study. Skills worth building include hand lettering, an understanding of proportion and spacing, font software, historical research and patient iteration. This month, draw the letters of one word that matters to you in a consistent style, refine the spacing three times, and note every decision you make about weight and curve." }
    ],
    ISFJ: [
      { zh: "纺织创作可以在纺织设计、织造或印花纺织，以及部分纯艺术课程中学习，可以留意编织、染色和刺绣设备完善的地方。值得培养的能力包括图案、调色、针法、植物染，以及系统地制作小样。这个月，选一块对你承载着记忆的布料，做一本包含十块小样的样品册，通过针法、颜色或肌理去回应它。", en: "Textile practice is taught in textile design, woven or printed textiles and some fine art courses; look for good weaving, dyeing and embroidery facilities. Skills worth building include pattern, colour mixing, stitch techniques, natural dyeing and systematic sampling. This month, choose a fabric that holds a memory for you and make a sample book of ten small swatches that respond to it through stitch, colour or texture." },
      { zh: "科学插画属于插画课程，或自然历史、医学插画等专门方向，有时也出现在设有艺术选修的理科专业中。值得培养的能力包括观察写生、准确的比例、水彩或数字绘制、标注，以及阅读科学资料。这个月，每周对着实物画同一株植物或同一件标本，记下它的变化。月底，这四张画就组成了一份关于生长的小研究。", en: "Scientific illustration sits within illustration courses, natural history or medical illustration specialisms, and sometimes science degrees with art options. Skills worth building include observational drawing, accurate proportion, watercolour or digital rendering, labelling and reading scientific sources. This month, draw the same plant or specimen from life once a week, noting its changes. By the end of the month, the four drawings form a small study of growth." },
      { zh: "书籍设计通常从印刷工坊完善的平面设计、字体排印和出版类课程中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括网格系统、文字排版、图片编排、纸张与装帧知识，以及与印厂沟通。这个月，为一本你喜欢的书重新设计版式，比如某一章的开篇，选择新的字体、页边距和纸张，打印、折好，拿在手里与原书对比一下。", en: "Book design grows from graphic design, typography and publishing courses with good print workshops. Skills worth building include grid systems, typesetting, image placement, knowledge of paper and binding, and working with printers. This month, redesign the layout of a book you love, perhaps one chapter opening, choosing a new typeface, margins and paper. Print it, fold it, and compare it in your hands with the original." }
    ],
    ESTJ: [
      { zh: "制作统筹把电影、摄影、戏剧和展览类课程与艺术管理连接起来，可以留意有真实制作项目的专业。值得培养的能力包括排期、预算、通告单、健康与安全意识，以及和供应商谈判。这个月，主动帮同学的一次拍摄或一场小活动管理后勤，做一张列出时间、分工、设备和联系人的通告单。结束后，记下你下次会怎样规划得不同。", en: "Production management connects film, photography, theatre and exhibition courses with arts management; look for programmes with live productions. Skills worth building include scheduling, budgeting, call sheets, health and safety awareness and negotiating with suppliers. This month, volunteer to manage logistics for a classmate’s shoot or a small event, and create a call sheet listing times, roles, equipment and contacts. Afterwards, note what you would plan differently." },
      { zh: "产品设计可以在产品、工业和家具设计类课程中学习，最好有完善的工坊和用户研究训练。值得培养的能力包括手绘草图、计算机辅助建模、做原型、了解生产制造，以及与用户一起测试。这个月，找一件让你觉得别扭的日常物品，访谈三位使用者，用纸板做一个改进版本的粗略原型，再请这三个人试用。", en: "Product design is taught in product, industrial and furniture design courses, ideally with strong workshops and user research. Skills worth building include sketching, CAD modelling, prototyping, an understanding of manufacturing and testing with users. This month, find an everyday object that annoys you, interview three people who use it, and make a rough cardboard prototype of an improved version. Then test it with the same three people." },
      { zh: "艺术管理通常从艺术管理、文化政策，以及设有职业实践内容的创意专业中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括项目规划、预算、撰写资助申请、清楚的报告，以及理解文化机构如何运营。这个月，研究一家你欣赏的画廊或艺术机构：它的资金从哪里来，观众是谁，开展哪些项目，然后把这些整理成一页总结。", en: "Arts administration grows from arts management, cultural policy and creative degrees with a professional practice strand. Skills worth building include project planning, budgeting, funding applications, clear reporting and an understanding of how cultural organisations are run. This month, research one gallery or arts organisation you admire: find out how it is funded, who its audiences are and what programmes it runs, then summarise it on one page." }
    ],
    ESFJ: [
      { zh: "画廊管理融合了艺术管理、艺术史和策展研究，可以留意提供画廊实习或有学生自营空间的课程。值得培养的能力包括观众接待、艺术家联络、销售基础、布展协调和清楚的沟通。这个月，去帮忙一场学生展或本地展览，承担一个下午的前台接待，记下观众最常问的问题，再为以后的工作人员拟好答案。", en: "Gallery management combines arts management, art history and curatorial studies. Look for courses with gallery placements or student-run spaces. Skills worth building include visitor care, artist liaison, sales basics, installation coordination and clear communication. This month, help out with a student or local exhibition, take on front-of-house for one afternoon, and note the questions visitors ask most often, then draft answers for future staff." },
      { zh: "艺术节制作把艺术管理、活动策划与带有真实项目的表演或策展类课程连接起来。值得培养的能力包括节目编排、志愿者协调、时间线、预算、宣传推广和应急预案。这个月，在纸上策划一场一天的迷你艺术节，比如为你的学校或社区，列出五场活动的日程、一张志愿者排班表，以及下雨时的备用方案。", en: "Festival production connects arts management, events, and performance or curatorial courses that run live projects. Skills worth building include programming, volunteer coordination, timelines, budgets, marketing and contingency planning. This month, plan a one-day mini festival on paper, perhaps for your school or neighbourhood, with a schedule of five events, a volunteer rota and a back-up plan in case it rains. Then ask a friend to spot the weak point." },
      { zh: "美育项目融合了艺术史、博物馆学和教育，有时还需要教师培训，可以留意提供博物馆教育部门实习的课程。值得培养的能力包括为不同年龄设计活动、通俗易懂的写作、带领导览和评估活动效果。这个月，选一件艺术作品，围绕它设计一个十五分钟的亲子活动，包括一个问题、一项动手任务和一张带回家的卡片，找一个你认识的家庭试一试。", en: "Museum learning draws on art history, museum studies and education, sometimes with teacher training. Look for courses with placements in learning departments. Skills worth building include designing activities for different ages, accessible writing, leading tours and evaluating sessions. This month, choose one artwork and design a fifteen-minute family activity around it, with a question, a hands-on task and a take-home card. Try it with a family you know." }
    ],
    ISTP: [
      { zh: "材料研究出现在材料未来、产品设计、纺织和雕塑类课程中，常常还会与科学领域合作。值得培养的能力包括系统测试、记录结果、理解可持续性、工坊安全，以及用视觉方式呈现发现。这个月，选一种废弃材料，比如咖啡渣或包装，测试五种黏合或重塑它的方法，每个结果都配上照片和测量数据。", en: "Material research sits within material futures, product design, textiles and sculpture courses, often alongside collaborations with scientists. Skills worth building include systematic testing, recording results, an understanding of sustainability, workshop safety and presenting findings visually. This month, choose one waste material, such as coffee grounds or packaging, test five ways to bind or reshape it, and record each result with a photograph and measurements." },
      { zh: "雕塑与制作适合工坊设备出色的纯艺术课程，以及三维设计和工艺类专业。值得培养的能力包括焊接、铸造、木工、数字化制造、结构思维和安全的工坊习惯。这个月，设计一件必须用单点站立的小雕塑，用废料做一个小样，测试需要做出哪些改动才能让它保持稳定，并把每次失败拍下来。", en: "Sculpture and fabrication suit fine art courses with excellent workshops, as well as three-dimensional design and craft programmes. Skills worth building include welding, casting, woodwork, digital fabrication, structural thinking and safe workshop practice. This month, design a small sculpture that must balance on a single point, make a maquette from scrap materials, and test what changes are needed to keep it stable, photographing each failure along the way." },
      { zh: "摄影指导可以在摄影机与灯光设备完善的电影、动态影像和摄影类课程中学习。值得培养的能力包括镜头选择、曝光、三点布光、调色基础，以及与导演合作。这个月，用三种不同的布光方式，比如自然光、单盏台灯和混合光源，把同一段三十秒的场景拍三遍，再比较氛围发生了怎样的变化。", en: "Cinematography is taught within film, moving-image and photography courses with good camera and lighting kit. Skills worth building include lens choice, exposure, three-point lighting, the basics of colour grading and working with a director. This month, film the same thirty-second scene three times with different lighting set-ups, such as daylight, a single lamp and mixed sources, then compare how the mood changes from one to the next." }
    ],
    ISFP: [
      { zh: "绘画可以在纯艺术课程中学习，有些课程还设有专门的绘画方向，可以留意工作室空间宽敞、导师辅导规律的地方。值得培养的能力包括色彩理论、观察写生、底材准备、系列创作，以及书写自己的创作。这个月，只用四种颜色，在一天中三个不同的时间画同一处小景，记下你的决定如何随光线变化。", en: "Painting is taught within fine art courses, some with a dedicated painting pathway; look for generous studio space and regular tutorials. Skills worth building include colour theory, observational drawing, surface preparation, working in series and writing about your practice. This month, paint the same small view at three different times of day, using a limited palette of four colours, and note how your decisions shift with the light." },
      { zh: "时装设计可以在时装设计、女装、男装或针织类课程，以及更宽泛的纺织专业中学习。值得培养的能力包括制版、立体裁剪、缝纫、面料知识、时装画和设计研究。这个月，只凭手感收集十块面料小样，可以的话闭上眼睛挑，再选出其中一块，披在人台或朋友身上，拍下它自然形成的五种造型。", en: "Fashion design is studied in fashion design, womenswear, menswear or knitwear courses, and in broader textile programmes. Skills worth building include pattern cutting, draping, sewing, fabric knowledge, fashion illustration and research. This month, collect ten fabric samples by touch alone, eyes closed if you can, then choose one and drape it on a dress form or a friend, photographing five shapes it naturally wants to make." },
      { zh: "陶艺可以在陶艺、工艺和三维设计类课程中学习，有时也是纯艺术的一个方向，可以留意窑炉使用方便、有技术员支持的地方。值得培养的能力包括拉坯、手捏成型、釉料化学、烧制和耐心的测试。这个月，用同一种泥做十个捏塑小罐，每次只改变一件事，比如胎壁厚度或表面痕迹，并记下每一个拿在手里的感觉。", en: "Ceramics is taught in ceramics, craft and three-dimensional design courses, and sometimes as a fine art pathway; look for good kiln access and technician support. Skills worth building include throwing, hand-building, glaze chemistry, firing and patient testing. This month, make ten small pinch pots from the same clay, varying one thing each time, such as wall thickness or surface mark, and keep notes on how each one feels in the hand." }
    ],
    ESTP: [
      { zh: "行为艺术适合欢迎现场与时基作品的纯艺术课程，以及表演与戏剧创作类专业。值得培养的能力包括身体觉察、节奏感、即兴、用照片和视频记录，以及读懂观众。这个月，只用一件物品设计一段三分钟的行动，表演给几位朋友看并录下来，再看录像，看看观众实际看到的是什么。", en: "Performance art fits fine art courses that welcome live and time-based work, as well as performance and theatre-making programmes. Skills worth building include body awareness, timing, improvisation, documentation through photography and video, and reading an audience. This month, devise a three-minute action using only one object, perform it for a few friends, film it, and watch the recording to see what the audience actually saw." },
      { zh: "纪实摄影可以在重视田野工作与伦理的摄影和新闻摄影类课程中学习。值得培养的能力包括快速操作相机、自然光拍摄、编辑图片序列、撰写图片说明，以及尊重地征得被摄者同意。这个月，从头到尾记录一场本地活动，至少拍两百张，再精选成一组十张、配有简短说明的图片故事。", en: "Documentary photography is taught within photography and photojournalism courses that value fieldwork and ethics. Skills worth building include fast camera handling, available-light shooting, editing a sequence, captioning and asking people for consent respectfully. This month, document one local event from start to finish, shoot at least two hundred frames, then edit them down to a story of ten images with short captions." },
      { zh: "舞台美术可以在戏剧设计、表演设计和美术设计类课程中学习，常常要与导演和演员合作。值得培养的能力包括制作舞台模型箱、技术制图、布景绘制、灯光意识，以及在排练中快速调整。这个月，从一部戏剧或电影里挑一个短场景，用鞋盒为它做一个比例模型，再用手机电筒给它打光。", en: "Set design is taught in theatre design, performance design and production design courses, often in collaboration with directors and performers. Skills worth building include model boxes, technical drawing, scenic painting, lighting awareness and adapting quickly during rehearsals. This month, choose a short scene from a play or film, build a scale model of a set for it in a shoebox, and light it with a phone torch." }
    ],
    ESFP: [
      { zh: "造型通常从时装传播、时装推广和摄影类课程，以及更宽泛的时装专业中成长起来。值得培养的能力包括视觉研究、寻找单品、对色彩与廓形的敏感、与摄影师合作，以及建立造型作品集。这个月，用自己的衣橱或慈善二手店里的单品搭出三套完整造型，每套讲一个不同角色的故事，拍下来，并为每套写一句话。", en: "Styling grows from fashion communication, fashion promotion and photography courses, as well as broader fashion programmes. Skills worth building include visual research, sourcing, a sense of colour and silhouette, working with photographers and building a styling portfolio. This month, create three complete looks from your own wardrobe or a charity shop, each telling a different character’s story, photograph them, and write one line for each." },
      { zh: "导演可以在电影、动态影像和动画类课程，以及音乐影像或广告方向中学习。值得培养的能力包括分镜、与演员合作、镜头规划、剪辑节奏，以及在现场给出清楚的指令。这个月，为一首你喜欢的歌拍一支六十秒的音乐影像，只用一部手机、三个地点，并至少请一位朋友出镜，剪完后放给另一位朋友看。", en: "Directing is taught within film, moving-image and animation courses, and in music video or advertising strands. Skills worth building include storyboarding, working with performers, shot planning, editing rhythm and giving clear direction on set. This month, make a sixty-second music video for a track you love, using only a phone, three locations and at least one friend in front of the camera, then show the edit to someone new." },
      { zh: "橱窗陈列设计融合了空间设计、视觉陈列、舞台美术和时装传播类课程。值得培养的能力包括三维构图、道具制作、灯光、为路人讲故事，以及在很短的布展时间内完成工作。这个月，选家里的一层架子或一处窗台，设计一个能在五秒内讲完故事的陈列，每周换一次，并从外侧为每个版本拍照。", en: "Display design draws on spatial design, visual merchandising, set design and fashion communication courses. Skills worth building include composition in three dimensions, prop making, lighting, storytelling for passers-by and working within tight installation slots. This month, choose a shelf or windowsill at home and design a display that tells a story in five seconds, change it weekly, and photograph each version from the street side." }
    ]
  };

  if (typeof module === "object" && module.exports) module.exports = L;
})(typeof window !== "undefined" ? window : globalThis);
